WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:03.968 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.968 --> 00:00:06.448 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.448 --> 00:00:16.540 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.760 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON:
OK, as I said, so,

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:24.545 align:middle line:84%
classic example here, American
Repertory Theater in Cambridge,

00:00:24.545 --> 00:00:26.170 align:middle line:84%
plays that are going
to go to Broadway,

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:29.650 align:middle line:84%
they try them out there, also
sort of avant garde stuff.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.619 align:middle line:84%
If I buy a ticket
there, it's like $72

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:35.580 align:middle line:84%
to sit in the back
corner of the theater.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.080 align:middle line:84%
You, as students, they actually,
if you go on their website,

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.247 align:middle line:84%
they have a tremendous
number of students, basically

00:00:40.247 --> 00:00:42.670 align:middle line:90%
everyone except me.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:46.300 align:middle line:84%
And the student
discount there, you

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.850 align:middle line:84%
can go see plays that cost me
$72 and you pay, like, $10.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.725 align:middle line:90%
It's less than going to a movie.

00:00:51.725 --> 00:00:53.350 align:middle line:84%
I would say, it's a
great thing to see,

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.900 align:middle line:84%
that it is typical, though,
that you might buy a ticket

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.160 align:middle line:84%
to see Hamlet and find that
you've got guys standing

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.430 align:middle line:84%
in kiddie bathtubs, wearing
shorts, playing guitars,

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:04.069 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:07.035 align:middle line:84%
So it is-- you have to
be aware that you may not

00:01:07.035 --> 00:01:08.410 align:middle line:84%
get what you think
you're getting

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.910 align:middle line:84%
when you see the title of the
play, but a great thing to go.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.600 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, example
of this, they're

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.680 align:middle line:84%
selling the same
ticket to you for $10

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.650 align:middle line:90%
they would charge me $72 for.

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.190 align:middle line:84%
People often also
think about this

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.522 align:middle line:90%
when you have nonlinear prices.

00:01:21.522 --> 00:01:22.730 align:middle line:90%
You'll go to the supermarket.

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.620 align:middle line:84%
And it's, like, buy
one, get one half off.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.200 align:middle line:84%
It's like, the
first good is being

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.490 align:middle line:84%
sold at a different price
from the second good.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.700 align:middle line:84%
And again, the first
thing of strawberries

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.050 align:middle line:84%
you buy and the second
thing of strawberries you

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:35.210 align:middle line:84%
buy, they're identical,
except they're selling them

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:38.660 align:middle line:90%
to you at different prices.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.270 align:middle line:84%
We also talk about
price discrimination

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:46.950 align:middle line:84%
when firms are selling similar
goods at very different prices.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.000 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, I put
the iPhone 14 down there.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.630 align:middle line:84%
The iPhone 14, 128-gigabyte
version is $799.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.140 align:middle line:84%
The 512-gigabyte
version is $1,099.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.603 align:middle line:84%
So it's $300 extra for
the more expensive iPhone.

00:02:04.603 --> 00:02:06.020 align:middle line:84%
You might think
to yourself, wait,

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:10.520 align:middle line:84%
I can buy a 512-gigabyte
flash drive for $50.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.107 align:middle line:84%
Why does it cost $300 more
to put it in the phone?

00:02:13.107 --> 00:02:13.940 align:middle line:90%
Is it better memory?

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:18.110 align:middle line:84%
No, it's exactly the same memory
that's in the $50 flash drive

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:19.530 align:middle line:84%
that someone's
making a profit on.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.460 align:middle line:84%
But somehow, two
similar goods are just

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:24.010 align:middle line:84%
being sold at very
different markups.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:29.330 align:middle line:90%


00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:32.720 align:middle line:84%
Again, whether we
think of something

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.430 align:middle line:84%
as price discrimination
when the goods differ,

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.590 align:middle line:84%
do you talk about
discrimination if it's, like,

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:41.870 align:middle line:84%
the ratio of the prices
to costs are different,

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.780 align:middle line:84%
or if the dollar value of
price to cost is different?

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.790 align:middle line:84%
But it is-- often
in these examples,

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:48.980 align:middle line:84%
it's very, very clear
that, however you

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.000 align:middle line:84%
think about adjusting
for the cost,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.250 align:middle line:84%
something is just much more
expensive than the others.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.792 align:middle line:84%
We'll call that
price discrimination.

00:02:54.792 --> 00:02:58.550 align:middle line:84%
The business class seat is
$12,000 when the coach seat is

00:02:58.550 --> 00:02:59.900 align:middle line:90%
$600 or whatever.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.390 align:middle line:84%
It's like, I don't know whether
I'm counting cubic inches

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:02.930 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:06.620 align:middle line:84%
But one is just
much more expensive.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:08.930 align:middle line:84%
Also, sometimes, you
can think about it

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.400 align:middle line:84%
as being price discrimination
when costs differ,

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.540 align:middle line:84%
and you don't
discriminate in prices.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.200 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, there are
places that offer free delivery.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.090 align:middle line:84%
And the free delivery
doesn't depend

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.152 align:middle line:90%
on how far it is to get to you.

00:03:22.152 --> 00:03:24.110 align:middle line:84%
So you might think that
they're, in some sense,

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:27.045 align:middle line:84%
when the firm is incurring a
large delivery cost to get it

00:03:27.045 --> 00:03:29.420 align:middle line:84%
to you, they're giving you a
discount, relative to people

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:31.610 align:middle line:84%
for whom they're spending
a small amount of money

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:32.730 align:middle line:90%
to get it to you.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:35.990 align:middle line:84%
And so, again, I would think of
that as charging the same price

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:38.240 align:middle line:84%
can actually be
price discrimination,

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.190 align:middle line:84%
if the costs are very
different of serving

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:42.020 align:middle line:90%
the different consumers.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:45.170 align:middle line:84%
OK, obviously,
price discrimination

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:46.470 align:middle line:90%
requires market power.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.470 align:middle line:84%
This was actually one
of my favorite signs

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:52.640 align:middle line:84%
I found at the Whole Foods once,
which was cranberries for sale,

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.707 align:middle line:84%
regularly $2, now 2 for
$4, which, I didn't quite

00:03:57.707 --> 00:03:58.915 align:middle line:90%
know what was going on there.

00:03:58.915 --> 00:04:01.520 align:middle line:90%


00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.150 align:middle line:84%
OK, so, theory of price
discrimination goes back

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:07.490 align:middle line:90%
to Pigou in the 1920s.

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:10.310 align:middle line:84%
Pigou made up three names
for three different types

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.850 align:middle line:90%
of price discrimination.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.460 align:middle line:90%
These names are terrible names.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:14.910 align:middle line:90%
But they've survived.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:18.680 align:middle line:84%
So, first one, he called first
degree price discrimination,

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:21.810 align:middle line:84%
which some people also refer to
as perfect price discrimination.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:27.260 align:middle line:84%
This is the ideal of a theorist
where the firm knows everything

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:28.020 align:middle line:90%
they need to know.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.370 align:middle line:84%
They have all the
power they would want.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:31.290 align:middle line:84%
They can price separately
to every consumer.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.753 align:middle line:84%
It's really a theoretical
ideal because firms are never

00:04:33.753 --> 00:04:36.170 align:middle line:84%
in the situation of exactly
knowing the consumer's utility

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:38.360 align:middle line:90%
function.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.990 align:middle line:84%
Second degree price
discrimination

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:45.060 align:middle line:84%
is price discrimination
based on self-selection.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.450 align:middle line:84%
So this would be
offering an iPhone

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.840 align:middle line:84%
14 in two different
quality levels

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.420 align:middle line:84%
and relying on the consumers
to sort themselves between,

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:58.280 align:middle line:84%
do they buy the cheap iPhone
14 or the expensive iPhone 14?

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:02.690 align:middle line:84%
And you see lots of other
examples of this, cell phone

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:04.970 align:middle line:84%
plans, health insurance
contracts, when you

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:07.580 align:middle line:90%
buy regular premium gasoline.

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:10.430 align:middle line:84%
Firms are setting these
prices, relying entirely

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:12.800 align:middle line:84%
on the consumers to
self-select among them.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.330 align:middle line:84%
And then third degree
price discrimination

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:19.030 align:middle line:84%
is the examples like
the ART student tickets

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:23.560 align:middle line:84%
or military discount or senior
citizen discount or whatever,

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:27.910 align:middle line:84%
where there are some
observable characteristics

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:29.110 align:middle line:90%
of the consumers.

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:31.067 align:middle line:84%
And then the firms can
set different prices

00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:32.650 align:middle line:84%
based on the different
characteristics

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:34.030 align:middle line:90%
of the consumers.

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:36.070 align:middle line:84%
Another one you'd see
here, classic third degree

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.740 align:middle line:84%
discrimination, is
geographic location.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:41.410 align:middle line:84%
Firms will set different
prices in different countries

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:44.200 align:middle line:84%
because they can
observe in which country

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.470 align:middle line:90%
someone is buying the good.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.890 align:middle line:84%
I would say, why is
this a bad nomenclature?

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.780 align:middle line:84%
One is, names first,
second, and third degree

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.580 align:middle line:90%
don't really have any meaning.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.580 align:middle line:84%
And then, besides not
really having any meaning,

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:58.990 align:middle line:90%
the order is wrong.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:02.020 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, first
degree is when you know

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:03.310 align:middle line:90%
everything about the consumer.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.810 align:middle line:84%
Third degree is when you know
something about the consumers.

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.227 align:middle line:84%
Second degree is when you know
nothing about the consumer.

00:06:08.227 --> 00:06:10.220 align:middle line:84%
So they really ought
to be in this order.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:13.730 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, that's the order
Pigou described them in.

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:16.800 align:middle line:84%
People still talk about second
degree price discrimination.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.430 align:middle line:84%
So, anyway, I'm
going to continue

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:23.270 align:middle line:84%
to use those names because
that's what everyone else uses.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:26.900 align:middle line:84%
So let me start with first
degree price discrimination.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:29.370 align:middle line:84%
So, first degree
is the simplest.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:34.460 align:middle line:84%
So suppose this is the
omniscient monopolist,

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.440 align:middle line:84%
the monopolist,
powerful, whatever.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.460 align:middle line:84%
So, a monopolist, every
different consumer

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:45.200 align:middle line:84%
who walks into the store,
the monopolist knows, this

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:48.860 align:middle line:90%
is p1 of x, this is p2 of x.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:53.870 align:middle line:84%
So if customer 1
walks into the store,

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:56.090 align:middle line:84%
the monopolist knows, OK,
I'm facing customer 1.

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:58.940 align:middle line:84%
This is customer 1's
willingness to pay for x units,

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:00.380 align:middle line:90%
for any number, x.

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:04.395 align:middle line:84%
And then, customer 2
walks in the store.

00:07:04.395 --> 00:07:06.020 align:middle line:84%
This is customer 2's
willingness to pay

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:07.490 align:middle line:90%
for any number of units, x.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:10.010 align:middle line:90%


00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:13.110 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to suppose that
this monopolist is powerful.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:16.310 align:middle line:84%
It can set customer-specific
nonlinear prices.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:20.600 align:middle line:84%
And it can prevent resale
across the consumers.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:22.530 align:middle line:90%
So what's going to happen?

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:26.180 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine some
very complicated menu

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:27.762 align:middle line:90%
of prices you set to consumers.

00:07:27.762 --> 00:07:28.970 align:middle line:90%
You have them play some game.

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:30.690 align:middle line:84%
You have them do
something or other.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:33.830 align:middle line:84%
But at the end of the day,
suppose, in equilibrium,

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:39.440 align:middle line:84%
this consumer buys x1 units
and ends up paying x2--

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:40.730 align:middle line:90%
sorry, ends up paying t1.

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:45.240 align:middle line:84%
And this consumer buys x2
units and ends up paying t2.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:47.480 align:middle line:84%
If this is what's going
to happen in equilibrium

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:49.502 align:middle line:84%
under some very
complicated scheme,

00:07:49.502 --> 00:07:51.710 align:middle line:84%
you might as well just make
a direct take it or leave

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:53.070 align:middle line:90%
it offer to each consumer.

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:57.410 align:middle line:84%
So just offer consumer 1, either
you buy x1 units at a price t1

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:58.980 align:middle line:90%
or you get nothing at all.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:07:59.960 align:middle line:90%
You go to consumer 2.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:02.780 align:middle line:84%
Either you get x2
units at a price of t2

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:04.200 align:middle line:90%
or you get nothing at all.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.160 align:middle line:84%
And so, anything you can achieve
with a more complicated scheme,

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:08.600 align:middle line:84%
you can achieve
with these simple

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:10.850 align:middle line:84%
take it or leave it,
customer by customer take it

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:12.720 align:middle line:90%
or leave it schemes, OK?

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.720 align:middle line:84%
So that's what we're going
to think about doing,

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:16.610 align:middle line:84%
is just offering these
customer-specific

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:19.160 align:middle line:90%
take it or leave it schemes.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:24.830 align:middle line:84%
When is customer 1 going to
accept an offer that involves

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:29.270 align:middle line:90%
x1 units at a price of t1?

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:35.000 align:middle line:84%
Well, if that's my offer,
this is the maximum amount

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.440 align:middle line:84%
that the customer is
willing to pay for x1 units.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.398 align:middle line:84%
They're willing to pay
this for the first unit,

00:08:39.398 --> 00:08:42.080 align:middle line:84%
this for the second unit, this
for the third unit, and so on.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:45.080 align:middle line:84%
So the maximum they'll
pay for x1 units

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:48.790 align:middle line:84%
is just the integral from
0 to x of Pi of s(ds).

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:53.660 align:middle line:84%
Given that the customer is
going to accept that offer, if

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:56.100 align:middle line:84%
and only if Ti is
less than this,

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.340 align:middle line:84%
clearly, the best thing
to do is to set the price

00:08:58.340 --> 00:09:00.300 align:middle line:84%
as high as the consumer's
willingness to pay.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:02.420 align:middle line:84%
So you set Ti equals
the integral from 0

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:04.940 align:middle line:90%
to x, Pi of s(ds).

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:09.230 align:middle line:84%
And so, thinking about
the monopoly problem,

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:13.100 align:middle line:84%
you're just trying to
maximize the total amount.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:16.470 align:middle line:84%
You pick the number of units,
xi, to sell that consumer.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:19.470 align:middle line:84%
This is the price you're going
to charge when you sell x units.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:23.030 align:middle line:84%
And then this is the cost
of producing the units.

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:28.010 align:middle line:84%
So, clearly, I differentiate
that with respect to xi.

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:32.550 align:middle line:84%
The derivative of this with
respect to xi is just Pi of xi.

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:35.160 align:middle line:84%
The derivative of this
with respect to xi is c.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:37.850 align:middle line:84%
So the first order
condition is just, set

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:43.010 align:middle line:84%
xi-star, such that Pi
of xi-star equals c.

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:47.990 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, it becomes,
the optimal solution is,

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:51.180 align:middle line:84%
do exactly what would happen
with perfect competition.

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:54.080 align:middle line:84%
So if this is my
constant marginal cost,

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.990 align:middle line:84%
for every consumer, I just
figure out, how many units

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:05.570 align:middle line:84%
would they buy if I was setting
the price then equal to c.

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:09.110 align:middle line:90%
And I do this as x1-star.

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:11.810 align:middle line:90%
I do this as x2-star.

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:15.590 align:middle line:84%
And then the total
amount that I charge

00:10:15.590 --> 00:10:19.830 align:middle line:84%
them is just their
total utility they

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:22.160 align:middle line:84%
get from buying the number
of units that I sell them.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.810 align:middle line:90%


00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:30.400 align:middle line:84%
So, comments, quantities are
exactly the same as with perfect

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:31.930 align:middle line:90%
competition.

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:35.120 align:middle line:84%
First degree discrimination
is socially optimal.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.390 align:middle line:84%
Again, I'm ignoring
distributional effects.

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:40.180 align:middle line:84%
But it's just,
there's no failure

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:41.360 align:middle line:90%
to realize gains from trade.

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:45.100 align:middle line:84%
Every unit that is worth
c gets sold to somebody.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:47.800 align:middle line:84%
Notice also that,
rather than having

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:52.090 align:middle line:84%
this take it or leave
it offer bundle,

00:10:52.090 --> 00:10:53.860 align:middle line:84%
the monopolist
could also do this

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:58.450 align:middle line:84%
by just offering the
good at a price of c,

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:01.960 align:middle line:84%
and then having a fixed fee
that they add on top of that.

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:07.160 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, for this
consumer, what I can do is say,

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:10.450 align:middle line:84%
you have to pay me
this fixed fee, which

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:14.410 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
area of this region.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:16.180 align:middle line:84%
And then you can
buy as many units

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:18.430 align:middle line:90%
as you want at a cost of c.

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:22.840 align:middle line:84%
And so, for this
consumer, again, you

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:24.760 align:middle line:90%
have to pay me this fixed fee.

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:26.290 align:middle line:84%
And then you can
buy as many units

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:28.360 align:middle line:90%
as you want at a cost of c.

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:31.420 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, suppose you're
a cloud computing provider.

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:33.910 align:middle line:84%
And you have customers
who are going

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:36.850 align:middle line:84%
to be doing a lot of
calculations on your platform.

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:38.680 align:middle line:84%
What you just have
to do is, you want

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:42.970 align:middle line:84%
to charge all the cores that
they rent from you and all

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:45.670 align:middle line:84%
the memory they rent from
you at your marginal cost

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:48.485 align:middle line:84%
of providing cores and
rent to avoid distorting

00:11:48.485 --> 00:11:50.860 align:middle line:84%
what they do and make the
service as valuable as possible

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:51.730 align:middle line:90%
for them.

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:53.980 align:middle line:84%
And then you make the service
as valuable as possible.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:56.605 align:middle line:84%
And then you extract all of the
surplus they would have gotten,

00:11:56.605 --> 00:11:58.670 align:middle line:84%
using the service in
a fixed fee up front.

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:03.590 align:middle line:84%
So it costs you $2,743,000
to join my platform.

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:06.945 align:middle line:84%
And then you can rent computing
costs at a marginal cost.

00:12:06.945 --> 00:12:09.350 align:middle line:90%
It costs you $4.7 million.

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:11.870 align:middle line:84%
You just have a different price
for every consumer to join.

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:14.328 align:middle line:84%
And then, once they're there,
you set the price equal to c.

00:12:14.328 --> 00:12:18.467 align:middle line:90%


00:12:18.467 --> 00:12:20.050 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, you
could think of that

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:22.990 align:middle line:84%
as maybe that's what
happens at Disneyland.

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:26.530 align:middle line:84%
Disneyland, you pay $135
to get into the park.

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:28.240 align:middle line:90%
And then the rides are free.

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:31.000 align:middle line:84%
And the rides are free because
the marginal cost of providing

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:32.870 align:middle line:90%
a ride is essentially 0.

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:36.160 align:middle line:84%
So what you want to do
is make the rides free,

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:38.650 align:middle line:84%
make the park as valuable as
possible, and then extract it

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:40.900 align:middle line:90%
all with these fixed fees, OK?

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:44.620 align:middle line:84%
The difference between
Disneyland and this example

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:48.310 align:middle line:84%
is, Disneyland tends to charge
everyone the same price.

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:50.745 align:middle line:84%
Here, you would have a
customer-specific price.

00:12:50.745 --> 00:12:52.120 align:middle line:84%
Every family that
walks in, you'd

00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:55.660 align:middle line:84%
be like, oh, for you,
$833 to go to the park,

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:58.410 align:middle line:84%
for you, $445 to
get into the park.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:00.410 align:middle line:84%
And every customer would
have a different price.

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:02.475 align:middle line:84%
But other than that,
the rides are free.

00:13:02.475 --> 00:13:06.360 align:middle line:90%


00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:09.610 align:middle line:84%
OK, questions on first
degree discrimination?

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:14.020 align:middle line:90%


00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:18.070 align:middle line:84%
In reality, there are a number
of reasons why we don't do this.

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:21.310 align:middle line:84%
One is, at least before there
were these omniscient internet

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:24.980 align:middle line:84%
firms, consumer preferences
are typically not observable.

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:27.320 align:middle line:84%
We don't know what the goods
are worth to the consumers.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:30.030 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, we can't do these
individual, personalized prices.

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:32.620 align:middle line:90%


00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:37.600 align:middle line:84%
Another constraint is arbitrage
or resale across consumers.

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:39.220 align:middle line:90%
So, imagine you did--

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:43.420 align:middle line:90%


00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:45.700 align:middle line:90%
Netflix is $12.95 a month.

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.310 align:middle line:84%
And you get to watch an
unlimited number of movies.

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:51.443 align:middle line:84%
You have the problem that,
instead of the one person

00:13:51.443 --> 00:13:53.860 align:middle line:84%
watching an unlimited number
of movies for $12.95 a month,

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:55.600 align:middle line:84%
all their friends
and their relatives

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:58.120 align:middle line:84%
can all use the same password
and all log in to Netflix

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:00.600 align:middle line:84%
and all watch an unlimited
number of movies per month.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.580 align:middle line:84%
So you need to be
able to prevent people

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:06.280 align:middle line:84%
from reselling the
good across consumers

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:10.710 align:middle line:84%
and having different people
consuming off the same pattern.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.230 align:middle line:84%
You could also
imagine, you might

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:13.940 align:middle line:84%
want to have something
like-- it seems

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:17.360 align:middle line:84%
like, with these
statistical packages,

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:20.330 align:middle line:84%
I can get this much power
for a very low price.

00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:25.280 align:middle line:84%
And then, for a low price, you
can get a low number of units.

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:28.207 align:middle line:84%
And then, for a high price,
you can get many more units.

00:14:28.207 --> 00:14:30.290 align:middle line:84%
You don't want someone
just coming up to you twice

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:31.970 align:middle line:84%
and buying the good,
and then showing

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:33.845 align:middle line:84%
up again half an hour
later and say, OK, now,

00:14:33.845 --> 00:14:37.820 align:middle line:84%
I'd like more units
also at the low price.

00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:39.770 align:middle line:90%
And then administrative costs.

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:43.190 align:middle line:84%
If you think about, where does
price discrimination occur,

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:46.670 align:middle line:84%
in the United States,
many, many goods

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:49.287 align:middle line:84%
are sold in stores that
have prices printed on them.

00:14:49.287 --> 00:14:50.120 align:middle line:90%
And you can walk in.

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:51.140 align:middle line:90%
And you buy the good.

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:53.210 align:middle line:90%
And you go home with it.

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:55.440 align:middle line:84%
You go to many
developing countries,

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:57.120 align:middle line:90%
that's not the way stores work.

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:00.320 align:middle line:84%
You walk into a store,
or a guy's shop.

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:03.750 align:middle line:84%
And you want to buy something,
especially if you're a tourist,

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:07.350 align:middle line:84%
and they just start discussing
what the price will be.

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:08.780 align:middle line:84%
And there is a
long haggling time.

00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:10.155 align:middle line:84%
And then, at the
end of haggling,

00:15:10.155 --> 00:15:12.710 align:middle line:84%
you eventually get to
buy what you want to buy.

00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:14.840 align:middle line:90%
Why does that happen?

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:17.510 align:middle line:84%
I would say, probably,
it's the low cost

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:19.950 align:middle line:84%
of labor in developing
country retail.

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:23.390 align:middle line:84%
There's just tremendous
excess employees there.

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:26.580 align:middle line:84%
You have these excess employees
whose time is worth almost 0.

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:29.330 align:middle line:84%
They can do the hard work of
trying to price discriminate,

00:15:29.330 --> 00:15:33.020 align:middle line:84%
figure out what it's worth to
you, make some extra money.

00:15:33.020 --> 00:15:36.380 align:middle line:84%
In the US, we actually
we do this for--

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:40.820 align:middle line:84%
we do haggle over cars and
over houses and things, where

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:42.440 align:middle line:90%
it's like, it's a car.

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:44.688 align:middle line:90%
The car is $30,000.

00:15:44.688 --> 00:15:46.730 align:middle line:84%
That's enough of a difference
that it makes sense

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:49.460 align:middle line:84%
to have a salesperson who's
trained in negotiating

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:52.070 align:middle line:84%
and figuring out how much the
customer's willingness to pay

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:53.870 align:middle line:84%
and trying to get them
to pay a little bit more,

00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:55.745 align:middle line:84%
or pay them a little
less, or get them to pay

00:15:55.745 --> 00:15:57.180 align:middle line:90%
a little less if they need to.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:01.748 align:middle line:84%
But normally, most
goods, it's not worth it.

00:16:01.748 --> 00:16:04.040 align:middle line:84%
I think there's a thought
that price discrimination may

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:08.480 align:middle line:84%
have gone up with the rise
of the internet because a bot

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:10.520 align:middle line:84%
that prices things in
a discriminatory way

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:12.800 align:middle line:84%
doesn't charge by the
hour for its work,

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:17.570 align:middle line:84%
trying to figure out how to
price discriminate among people.

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:20.630 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, you can come
up with several examples

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:23.570 align:middle line:84%
where, if these
problems are severe,

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:25.230 align:middle line:84%
price discrimination
is impossible.

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:27.470 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance,
simplest example here,

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:29.960 align:middle line:84%
suppose customers
have unit demands.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:33.200 align:middle line:84%
And there's a competitive
resale market.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:37.550 align:middle line:84%
If there's a competitive resale
market, then in that resale

00:16:37.550 --> 00:16:42.380 align:middle line:84%
market, the price of the good is
going to end up being capital P,

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:48.030 align:middle line:84%
where P is the inverse
demand for aggregate demand.

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:50.360 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be P of
the total number of units

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:52.550 align:middle line:90%
that got sold in equilibrium.

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:56.840 align:middle line:84%
And so, given that consumers
can always buy this good at P*

00:16:56.840 --> 00:16:59.120 align:middle line:84%
in the resale market,
they're going to buy it from

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:02.000 align:middle line:84%
the monopolist, if and
only if the number of units

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:06.800 align:middle line:84%
the monopolist is selling them
is less than P* times the number

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:08.630 align:middle line:84%
of units they get,
if that's the price.

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:12.060 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, you might as well
just directly sell it for P*.

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:13.360 align:middle line:90%
And there's nothing you can do.

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:17.030 align:middle line:90%


00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:20.420 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, I put a couple
other examples on there.

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:25.714 align:middle line:84%
Here, I always like finding
examples of factors limiting

00:17:25.714 --> 00:17:27.589 align:middle line:84%
price discrimination
and how, sometimes, it's

00:17:27.589 --> 00:17:29.422 align:middle line:84%
harder to price
discriminate than you think.

00:17:29.422 --> 00:17:30.840 align:middle line:90%
And sometimes, it's easier.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:32.940 align:middle line:84%
So here's the, it's
harder than you think.

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:35.840 align:middle line:84%
There's a story of an
Italian supermarket

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:39.680 align:middle line:84%
that had a 20% discount on
groceries for anyone over 60.

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:41.180 align:middle line:84%
And they thought
that this was going

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:44.930 align:middle line:84%
to be a fantastic plan
because senior citizens are

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:45.810 align:middle line:90%
more price-sensitive.

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:47.310 align:middle line:84%
They're willing to
shop around more.

00:17:47.310 --> 00:17:48.690 align:middle line:90%
They may go to multiple stores.

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:50.300 align:middle line:84%
We'll just offer
them all a discount.

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:51.870 align:middle line:90%
And they will buy the goods.

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:54.470 align:middle line:84%
And what they realized,
apparently only after a year,

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:57.110 align:middle line:84%
was that a number of
seniors in that town

00:17:57.110 --> 00:18:00.270 align:middle line:84%
had started businesses of
shopping for other people.

00:18:00.270 --> 00:18:02.582 align:middle line:84%
And they had become
professional shoppers.

00:18:02.582 --> 00:18:04.290 align:middle line:84%
You would go hand them
your grocery list.

00:18:04.290 --> 00:18:06.470 align:middle line:84%
They would walk into the
store, buy all the goods,

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:09.350 align:middle line:84%
get the 20% discount,
and then split

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:12.350 align:middle line:84%
the money with the customers,
who were happy to--

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:14.750 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, it
was a forerunner

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:15.972 align:middle line:90%
to Instacart or whatever.

00:18:15.972 --> 00:18:17.180 align:middle line:90%
They would take the discount.

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:18.600 align:middle line:90%
They would give you the stuff.

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:21.200 align:middle line:84%
And then everyone was
everyone was better off.

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:23.887 align:middle line:84%
And I guess, when the firm
realized that, they decided,

00:18:23.887 --> 00:18:25.970 align:middle line:84%
maybe this wasn't such a
good price discrimination

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:27.590 align:middle line:90%
scheme after all.

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:30.465 align:middle line:84%
Second example, this
is a classic photo.

00:18:30.465 --> 00:18:31.590 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't come up so well.

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:34.048 align:middle line:84%
At least, if I turn off these
lights, you'll see it better.

00:18:34.048 --> 00:18:37.760 align:middle line:84%
But for those of you who are
familiar with the Dutch and

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:41.060 align:middle line:84%
French flags, the
Dutch and French flags

00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:44.000 align:middle line:84%
are very similar, although one
is, the stripes are vertical.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:45.930 align:middle line:84%
And the other, the
stripes are horizontal.

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:49.940 align:middle line:84%
But if you're a company that
decides to sell pillows online

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:52.760 align:middle line:84%
and you want to make
a Dutch flag pillow,

00:18:52.760 --> 00:18:55.790 align:middle line:84%
it would look kind of like this,
a red stripe, a white stripe,

00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:56.610 align:middle line:90%
and a blue stripe.

00:18:56.610 --> 00:18:58.770 align:middle line:84%
If you want to make
a French flag pillow,

00:18:58.770 --> 00:19:00.812 align:middle line:84%
it would look like a blue
stripe, a white stripe,

00:19:00.812 --> 00:19:03.140 align:middle line:90%
and a red stripe.

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:06.650 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, at some point,
they realized, well,

00:19:06.650 --> 00:19:09.530 align:middle line:84%
it's the same pillow,
just turned on its side.

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:13.260 align:middle line:84%
But somehow, on their website,
the website had the two of them

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:14.020 align:middle line:90%
there.

00:19:14.020 --> 00:19:16.140 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if Dutch
people pay less for pillows

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:17.070 align:middle line:90%
than French people do.

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:19.720 align:middle line:84%
But they had, the
Dutch pillow was $9.99.

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:21.580 align:middle line:90%
The French pillow was $15.99.

00:19:21.580 --> 00:19:23.805 align:middle line:84%
And I guess you had
to count on people

00:19:23.805 --> 00:19:25.680 align:middle line:84%
not realizing they could
buy the wrong pillow

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:28.920 align:middle line:84%
and just turn it sideways to
get the pillow that they--

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:30.780 align:middle line:84%
the French people could
get the Dutch pillow

00:19:30.780 --> 00:19:32.780 align:middle line:84%
and turn it on its side
and get it more cheaply.

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:35.910 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the reds and the
blues are not exactly the same

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:38.597 align:middle line:84%
on the three pillows,
on the flags themselves.

00:19:38.597 --> 00:19:40.680 align:middle line:84%
But I'm sure, if you bought
those pillows, they're

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:43.560 align:middle line:84%
the same pillow,
just turned sideways.

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:46.470 align:middle line:84%
And apparently,
arbitrage was not

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:49.310 align:middle line:84%
enough to prevent that scheme
from working to some degree.

00:19:49.310 --> 00:19:53.250 align:middle line:90%


00:19:53.250 --> 00:20:00.030 align:middle line:84%
OK, so, next step, third
degree discrimination.

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:04.920 align:middle line:84%
So, suppose the monopolist now
can distinguish between classes

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:09.520 align:middle line:84%
of consumers but is limited
to using simple linear pricing

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:10.270 align:middle line:90%
within each group.

00:20:10.270 --> 00:20:13.860 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, maybe this
pillow retailer actually

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:15.700 align:middle line:90%
has two different websites.

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:17.730 align:middle line:84%
They have a Dutch website
that's only in Dutch

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:20.050 align:middle line:84%
and a French website
that's only in French.

00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:21.902 align:middle line:84%
And so they can just
set different prices

00:20:21.902 --> 00:20:22.860 align:middle line:90%
in different countries.

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:25.483 align:middle line:84%
And consumers can't
see the other website.

00:20:25.483 --> 00:20:28.890 align:middle line:90%


00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:31.380 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, so I'm going
to have two groups.

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:32.440 align:middle line:90%
i equals 1 and 2.

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:35.370 align:middle line:84%
This will also be
students and non-students.

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:39.210 align:middle line:84%
Each group has a
demand, Xi, of p.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:41.940 align:middle line:84%
And then, again, I'm going to go
with the constant marginal cost

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:44.250 align:middle line:90%
of c.

00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:47.250 align:middle line:84%
Here, I think what I'm going
to do is think about group 1

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:50.820 align:middle line:84%
as being the students, or
the low demand consumers,

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:56.820 align:middle line:84%
and group 2 as being the
higher demand consumers.

00:20:56.820 --> 00:21:00.273 align:middle line:90%
OK, so this is population 2.

00:21:00.273 --> 00:21:03.000 align:middle line:90%


00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:04.410 align:middle line:90%
I put price in this axis.

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:06.000 align:middle line:90%
I guess I switched.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:09.390 align:middle line:90%
So this is x1 of p.

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:12.360 align:middle line:90%
This is x2 of p.

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:14.132 align:middle line:84%
So, at any price,
the students are

00:21:14.132 --> 00:21:15.840 align:middle line:84%
willing to pay less
than the non-students

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:18.780 align:middle line:84%
because, presumably,
they have less money.

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:22.770 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, with discrimination,
what are you going to do?

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:25.050 align:middle line:84%
Within each
population, i, you're

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:29.340 align:middle line:84%
just going to maximize pi
minus c times Xi of pi.

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:32.580 align:middle line:84%
This is just the
monopoly pricing problem.

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:35.130 align:middle line:84%
So the monopoly pricing problem
has the marginal revenue

00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:37.620 align:middle line:84%
equals marginal cost
first order condition.

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:41.040 align:middle line:84%
Price minus cost over price
is 1 over the negative 1

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:44.130 align:middle line:84%
over the elasticity of demand,
just like it was last week.

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:51.520 align:middle line:84%
And so, let me just say here,
let's suppose we have cost here.

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:54.630 align:middle line:90%
So this is p1*.

00:21:54.630 --> 00:21:57.480 align:middle line:90%
And then, this is cost.

00:21:57.480 --> 00:21:59.970 align:middle line:90%
This is p2*.

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:02.490 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to assume
that-- label the markets,

00:22:02.490 --> 00:22:04.770 align:middle line:84%
that the monopoly
price in market 1

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:07.175 align:middle line:84%
is smaller than the
monopoly price in market 2.

00:22:07.175 --> 00:22:10.780 align:middle line:90%


00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:13.060 align:middle line:84%
So third degree
discrimination, if you're

00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:16.120 align:middle line:84%
allowed to practice it, is
just monopoly pricing market

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:16.710 align:middle line:90%
by market.

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:22.760 align:middle line:90%


00:22:22.760 --> 00:22:24.470 align:middle line:84%
Interesting questions
that come up

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:26.720 align:middle line:84%
in third degree
discrimination is,

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:29.870 align:middle line:84%
what if you decide that
discrimination should not

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:34.140 align:middle line:84%
be allowed and we want to
ban price discrimination?

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:37.835 align:middle line:90%


00:22:37.835 --> 00:22:38.710 align:middle line:90%
You know what we say?

00:22:38.710 --> 00:22:41.350 align:middle line:84%
And generally, we have
a funny relationship

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:42.850 align:middle line:84%
with price
discrimination in the US.

00:22:42.850 --> 00:22:45.058 align:middle line:84%
So there is something called
the Robinson-Patman Act,

00:22:45.058 --> 00:22:48.310 align:middle line:84%
which dates to the
early 20th century.

00:22:48.310 --> 00:22:51.360 align:middle line:84%
Robinson-Patman Act bans
price discrimination.

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:54.120 align:middle line:84%
But it bans price
discrimination to the extent

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:56.050 align:middle line:90%
that it harms competition.

00:22:56.050 --> 00:22:58.590 align:middle line:84%
And so, it was originally
developed for things

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:03.990 align:middle line:84%
like not allowing the
railroads to charge

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:07.140 align:middle line:84%
one firm more for shipping oil
than another firm for shipping

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:09.420 align:middle line:84%
oil in order to create an
oil monopoly on the East

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:12.390 align:middle line:84%
Coast of the US, or
something like that.

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:17.850 align:middle line:84%
It's typically not applied to
retail price discrimination

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:19.530 align:middle line:84%
or price discrimination
that isn't

00:23:19.530 --> 00:23:22.140 align:middle line:84%
going to be harming
competition of something, where

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:26.340 align:middle line:84%
you're providing an intermediate
input to some downstream firms.

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:29.250 align:middle line:84%
But there's some times when we
think that price discrimination

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:30.510 align:middle line:90%
is OK.

00:23:30.510 --> 00:23:34.260 align:middle line:84%
Somehow, it's OK to give
senior citizen discounts.

00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:37.440 align:middle line:90%


00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:39.360 align:middle line:84%
It's OK to give
student discounts.

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:41.740 align:middle line:84%
But it's not OK to
charge people extra.

00:23:41.740 --> 00:23:43.173 align:middle line:90%
So I don't quite know how it is.

00:23:43.173 --> 00:23:44.340 align:middle line:90%
It's like, discounts are OK.

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:48.780 align:middle line:84%
Charging someone more would
be totally inappropriate.

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:52.703 align:middle line:84%
If you had a women's
surcharge that is,

00:23:52.703 --> 00:23:54.120 align:middle line:84%
we have a price
in our restaurant.

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:56.010 align:middle line:90%
And women have to pay 10% more.

00:23:56.010 --> 00:23:58.406 align:middle line:84%
That would be considered
completely inappropriate.

00:23:58.406 --> 00:24:02.520 align:middle line:90%


00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:08.400 align:middle line:84%
Or if there's a big
storm, we have a surcharge

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:11.760 align:middle line:84%
on gasoline or
firewood or whatever,

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:14.100 align:middle line:84%
because there's been
some natural disaster.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:16.900 align:middle line:84%
Discrimination there is just
completely inappropriate.

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:22.110 align:middle line:84%
And there are anti-price-gouging
laws that prevent that.

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:24.900 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, what are we
going to do in this market

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:28.403 align:middle line:84%
if you decide to ban
price discrimination?

00:24:28.403 --> 00:24:30.070 align:middle line:84%
Well, you're just
going to maximize over

00:24:30.070 --> 00:24:35.400 align:middle line:84%
a single price, p, p minus c,
x1 of p, plus p minus cX2 of p.

00:24:35.400 --> 00:24:38.770 align:middle line:90%


00:24:38.770 --> 00:24:41.520 align:middle line:84%
If you want to think about what
the first order condition looks

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:44.430 align:middle line:90%
like, you just have--

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:50.130 align:middle line:84%
the single good case we had
this was d-pi i-dp You just are

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:54.180 align:middle line:84%
adding that up across the
two markets and getting

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:57.210 align:middle line:84%
the derivative of profit with
respect to p in market 1,

00:24:57.210 --> 00:25:01.380 align:middle line:84%
plus d(pi)/d(p) in
market 2 equals 0.

00:25:01.380 --> 00:25:02.595 align:middle line:90%
What's that going to do?

00:25:02.595 --> 00:25:07.770 align:middle line:84%
The most standard result is
that, if demand is well-behaved,

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:11.370 align:middle line:84%
by which I mean two things,
demand is single-peaked

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:15.360 align:middle line:84%
and it's concave in p
when p is less than p2*,

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:17.940 align:middle line:84%
then what the firm does is,
when you ban discrimination,

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:20.020 align:middle line:84%
they just choose an
intermediate price.

00:25:20.020 --> 00:25:24.700 align:middle line:84%
The consumers, the students,
are hurt by the price going up.

00:25:24.700 --> 00:25:27.750 align:middle line:84%
The non-students are helped
by the price going down.

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:30.690 align:middle line:90%
And why is that going to happen?

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:31.865 align:middle line:90%
If you think about it--

00:25:31.865 --> 00:25:37.440 align:middle line:90%


00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:44.440 align:middle line:84%
OK, so, this thing has a-- this
was the monopoly price here.

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:53.160 align:middle line:84%
So if I look at d(pi1)/d(P),
that's what it looks like.

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:56.130 align:middle line:84%
d(pi)/d(P), if
profit is concave,

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:58.180 align:middle line:84%
then d-pi dp is
downward-sloping.

00:25:58.180 --> 00:26:01.200 align:middle line:90%
It crosses 0 at p1*.

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:08.790 align:middle line:84%
And in this example, if I
graph d(pi2)/d(P), again,

00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:10.060 align:middle line:90%
it's downward-sloping.

00:26:10.060 --> 00:26:11.520 align:middle line:90%
It crosses 0 at p2*.

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:14.480 align:middle line:90%


00:26:14.480 --> 00:26:16.280 align:middle line:84%
So then, if you ask,
well, what if I just

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:21.020 align:middle line:84%
add this one and this
one, I have one function

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:23.420 align:middle line:90%
that's going negative here.

00:26:23.420 --> 00:26:27.050 align:middle line:84%
I have another function
that's going negative here.

00:26:27.050 --> 00:26:32.090 align:middle line:84%
The optimal price, p*, is just
the amount by which this one is

00:26:32.090 --> 00:26:34.590 align:middle line:84%
negative equals the amount
by this one is positive,

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:37.430 align:middle line:84%
so that the sum of the two
first order conditions is 0.

00:26:37.430 --> 00:26:41.090 align:middle line:84%
And as I've drawn it,
here, this one is positive.

00:26:41.090 --> 00:26:42.200 align:middle line:90%
And this one's 0.

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:44.700 align:middle line:84%
Here, this one's positive,
and this one's negative.

00:26:44.700 --> 00:26:48.230 align:middle line:84%
So obviously, the p* is
going to be in between.

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:54.830 align:middle line:84%
And what I needed to
do to say that is that,

00:26:54.830 --> 00:26:58.280 align:middle line:84%
if these things are concave,
then those first order

00:26:58.280 --> 00:27:00.060 align:middle line:84%
conditions are both
downward-sloping.

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:02.227 align:middle line:84%
And so, when they're
downward-sloping, this happens.

00:27:02.227 --> 00:27:07.070 align:middle line:90%


00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:11.930 align:middle line:84%
Just one comment
on elasticities,

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:17.690 align:middle line:84%
still, we just have
this new demand, x of p,

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:23.150 align:middle line:84%
which is equal to x1
of p plus x2 of p.

00:27:23.150 --> 00:27:27.990 align:middle line:84%
Your inverse elasticity pricing
based on this aggregate demand--

00:27:27.990 --> 00:27:31.610 align:middle line:84%
and just to note, when you
look at an aggregate demand,

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:34.280 align:middle line:84%
the elasticity in
a market that's

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:36.710 align:middle line:84%
a component of two
sub-markets is just

00:27:36.710 --> 00:27:38.780 align:middle line:84%
a weighted average
of the elasticities

00:27:38.780 --> 00:27:41.052 align:middle line:90%
in the individual markets.

00:27:41.052 --> 00:27:43.010 align:middle line:84%
And it's a weighted
average of the elasticities

00:27:43.010 --> 00:27:46.730 align:middle line:84%
in the individual markets,
but where you're waiting

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:48.570 align:middle line:90%
by the share of the market.

00:27:48.570 --> 00:27:51.200 align:middle line:84%
So if there's a market that
has more customers buying

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:55.880 align:middle line:84%
at that price, it gets a larger
weight than one that's smaller.

00:27:55.880 --> 00:27:58.310 align:middle line:84%
So it is the case that this
is, again, another reason

00:27:58.310 --> 00:27:59.960 align:middle line:84%
to think about why
it's intermediate

00:27:59.960 --> 00:28:02.690 align:middle line:84%
is, you have the high
elasticity market.

00:28:02.690 --> 00:28:04.610 align:middle line:84%
You have the low
elasticity market.

00:28:04.610 --> 00:28:06.955 align:middle line:84%
The combined market has an
intermediate elasticity,

00:28:06.955 --> 00:28:08.830 align:middle line:84%
which is a weighted
average of the other two.

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:12.360 align:middle line:84%
So, therefore, it's
in between, OK?

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:15.630 align:middle line:90%


00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:18.193 align:middle line:84%
Well, this is what we think
of as the normal case.

00:28:18.193 --> 00:28:21.180 align:middle line:90%


00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:26.370 align:middle line:84%
I was assuming that
pi* is concave.

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:32.250 align:middle line:84%
Profit functions are never
concave, pretty much.

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:34.260 align:middle line:84%
What do profit
functions look like?

00:28:34.260 --> 00:28:38.370 align:middle line:90%
We normally draw them like this.

00:28:38.370 --> 00:28:40.710 align:middle line:84%
But we ignore the
fact that profits

00:28:40.710 --> 00:28:43.290 align:middle line:84%
don't go to negative infinity
as p goes to infinity.

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:46.300 align:middle line:84%
As p goes to infinity, people
stop buying your product.

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:49.290 align:middle line:84%
So what profit functions
look like in practice is,

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:50.430 align:middle line:90%
they go like this.

00:28:50.430 --> 00:28:53.730 align:middle line:84%
And then they asymptote to
0 as p goes to infinity.

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:56.970 align:middle line:84%
So they're concave
part of the time.

00:28:56.970 --> 00:29:01.430 align:middle line:84%
And then they become
convex, as p gets large.

00:29:01.430 --> 00:29:04.160 align:middle line:84%
And if you think
about what happens

00:29:04.160 --> 00:29:08.180 align:middle line:84%
when this is what
profits look like,

00:29:08.180 --> 00:29:12.620 align:middle line:84%
imagine what I have is the
profit in the low demand market

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:15.020 align:middle line:90%
looks like this.

00:29:15.020 --> 00:29:19.310 align:middle line:84%
And the profit in the
high demand market

00:29:19.310 --> 00:29:22.490 align:middle line:90%
looks like this, OK?

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:25.360 align:middle line:84%
What do I get when I
add those two together?

00:29:25.360 --> 00:29:27.400 align:middle line:84%
I get something
that goes like this.

00:29:27.400 --> 00:29:31.700 align:middle line:90%


00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:39.500 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, after
some point, when this one

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:41.390 align:middle line:84%
becomes essentially
0, this curve

00:29:41.390 --> 00:29:44.300 align:middle line:90%
just coincides with this curve.

00:29:44.300 --> 00:29:46.820 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm adding
together one profit that

00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:49.350 align:middle line:84%
looks like this and one
profit that looks like this,

00:29:49.350 --> 00:29:51.860 align:middle line:84%
there may be this bump
over here on the left side,

00:29:51.860 --> 00:29:54.440 align:middle line:84%
or maybe there isn't even
a bump on the left side.

00:29:54.440 --> 00:29:59.960 align:middle line:84%
But the maximizer here
can just coincide.

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:03.530 align:middle line:84%
If this one, imagine, no student
is willing to pay more than $100

00:30:03.530 --> 00:30:06.150 align:middle line:84%
for a ticket to the A.R.T.
or something like that.

00:30:06.150 --> 00:30:07.700 align:middle line:90%
Then this just hits 0.

00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:10.560 align:middle line:84%
When this hits 0,
these things coincide.

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:12.650 align:middle line:84%
And what you do is
you just abandon

00:30:12.650 --> 00:30:15.700 align:middle line:84%
market 1 and monopoly
price in market 2.

00:30:15.700 --> 00:30:19.890 align:middle line:90%


00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:26.970 align:middle line:84%
So if you have that case
where the concavity is not

00:30:26.970 --> 00:30:29.430 align:middle line:84%
applying in the relevant
region, you don't necessarily

00:30:29.430 --> 00:30:31.240 align:middle line:84%
get the intermediate
pricing result.

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:34.680 align:middle line:84%
You can get the just
price to market 2 result.

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:39.150 align:middle line:84%
If you just price to
market 2, then, clearly,

00:30:39.150 --> 00:30:42.390 align:middle line:84%
if when you price discriminate,
you just price to market 2

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:44.370 align:middle line:84%
and you eliminate
the student discount,

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:46.890 align:middle line:84%
then, clearly,
everybody is worse off.

00:30:46.890 --> 00:30:49.050 align:middle line:84%
The monopolist makes less
money because it's now

00:30:49.050 --> 00:30:50.913 align:middle line:84%
no longer selling
to any students.

00:30:50.913 --> 00:30:52.830 align:middle line:84%
The students are worse
off because they're not

00:30:52.830 --> 00:30:53.770 align:middle line:90%
buying any tickets.

00:30:53.770 --> 00:30:56.860 align:middle line:84%
They get zero surplus instead
of getting positive surplus.

00:30:56.860 --> 00:31:03.720 align:middle line:84%
So that's a case where there's a
clear profit and social welfare

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:05.640 align:middle line:84%
and consumer surplus
advantage to allowing

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:07.150 align:middle line:90%
price discrimination.

00:31:07.150 --> 00:31:12.800 align:middle line:90%


00:31:12.800 --> 00:31:15.710 align:middle line:84%
In general, the monopoly
result is fully general.

00:31:15.710 --> 00:31:17.660 align:middle line:84%
Monopolies always
benefit from price

00:31:17.660 --> 00:31:20.870 align:middle line:84%
discriminating because they
could always choose not to.

00:31:20.870 --> 00:31:23.420 align:middle line:84%
The monopolist could be told,
OK, this customer is in France.

00:31:23.420 --> 00:31:25.700 align:middle line:84%
This customer is
in the Netherlands.

00:31:25.700 --> 00:31:27.840 align:middle line:84%
They could just ignore that
and set the same price.

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:30.650 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that they choose
to set different prices

00:31:30.650 --> 00:31:33.020 align:middle line:84%
means they're always
better off discriminating.

00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:37.190 align:middle line:84%
But whether consumer surplus
and social welfare change,

00:31:37.190 --> 00:31:40.610 align:middle line:90%
that's a different story.

00:31:40.610 --> 00:31:42.440 align:middle line:84%
This normal case,
we have the sense

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:45.030 align:middle line:84%
that some consumers would like
you to stop discriminating.

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:47.420 align:middle line:84%
Some don't want you to
stop discriminating.

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:48.865 align:middle line:90%
But that's not always true.

00:31:48.865 --> 00:31:52.630 align:middle line:90%


00:31:52.630 --> 00:31:56.410 align:middle line:90%
OK, a little more about welfare.

00:31:56.410 --> 00:32:00.340 align:middle line:84%
So to think about aggregate
social welfare of price

00:32:00.340 --> 00:32:03.280 align:middle line:84%
discrimination, you
have to think about two

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:05.090 align:middle line:90%
different welfare effects.

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:08.920 align:middle line:84%
So, one welfare effect of
price discrimination, or not

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:11.150 align:middle line:84%
allowing price discrimination,
is deadweight loss.

00:32:11.150 --> 00:32:12.910 align:middle line:84%
How many consumers
are there who would

00:32:12.910 --> 00:32:14.530 align:middle line:84%
be willing to pay
what it cost, who

00:32:14.530 --> 00:32:18.640 align:middle line:84%
would like to buy the good at
cost, who don't get served?

00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:23.380 align:middle line:84%
And then, the new problem we
get in price discrimination

00:32:23.380 --> 00:32:26.590 align:middle line:84%
is misallocation of
goods sold, that when

00:32:26.590 --> 00:32:28.450 align:middle line:84%
you sell the goods
at different prices,

00:32:28.450 --> 00:32:32.300 align:middle line:84%
there are high value people
who are not buying the good.

00:32:32.300 --> 00:32:36.010 align:middle line:84%
And there are people with lower
values who are getting the good.

00:32:36.010 --> 00:32:41.290 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, let's
think of a hypothetical world

00:32:41.290 --> 00:32:45.070 align:middle line:84%
where, somehow,
Apple has two markets

00:32:45.070 --> 00:32:47.620 align:middle line:90%
to which they sell iPhones.

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:51.520 align:middle line:84%
They sell iPhones to people
who want to use them as phones.

00:32:51.520 --> 00:32:53.060 align:middle line:84%
And they also sell
iPhones to people

00:32:53.060 --> 00:32:55.280 align:middle line:84%
who want to use them
as paper weights.

00:32:55.280 --> 00:32:57.590 align:middle line:84%
And somehow, they
have this ability

00:32:57.590 --> 00:32:59.390 align:middle line:90%
to restrict you when you buy it.

00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:02.130 align:middle line:84%
I'm in the paperweight market
or I'm in the phone market.

00:33:02.130 --> 00:33:04.047 align:middle line:84%
And if you only buy in
the paperweight market,

00:33:04.047 --> 00:33:06.440 align:middle line:84%
they ensure that the phone
actually won't ever work.

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:07.740 align:middle line:90%
It's still a working phone.

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:09.560 align:middle line:84%
They just somehow
manage to block you

00:33:09.560 --> 00:33:11.840 align:middle line:90%
from ever getting service on it.

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:15.350 align:middle line:84%
If you imagine that world,
it would be a funny world

00:33:15.350 --> 00:33:17.960 align:middle line:84%
where you have some
people who would

00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:20.630 align:middle line:84%
like to pay $700 for
an iPhone and who

00:33:20.630 --> 00:33:24.500 align:middle line:84%
don't get to have an iPhone
because the price is $799.

00:33:24.500 --> 00:33:26.330 align:middle line:90%
And then you have other people--

00:33:26.330 --> 00:33:28.020 align:middle line:90%
or maybe it's a kids toy.

00:33:28.020 --> 00:33:31.700 align:middle line:84%
It's an iPhone kids toy that's
actually a functioning iPhone.

00:33:31.700 --> 00:33:34.800 align:middle line:90%
And they get to buy it for $50.

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:37.040 align:middle line:84%
And then, so you
have someone who

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:41.608 align:middle line:84%
has this value for
$50 for this toy who's

00:33:41.608 --> 00:33:43.400 align:middle line:84%
getting to use the toy,
and someone someone

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:45.960 align:middle line:84%
who's buying the phone
at $700 isn't getting it.

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:47.803 align:middle line:84%
There's this gains
from trade where,

00:33:47.803 --> 00:33:49.220 align:middle line:84%
if the person
owning the toy phone

00:33:49.220 --> 00:33:51.740 align:middle line:84%
could hand it to the person
who wants to use the phone,

00:33:51.740 --> 00:33:55.550 align:middle line:84%
there'd be this big
gain in social surplus.

00:33:55.550 --> 00:34:02.300 align:middle line:84%
But you're going to get that any
time you have a senior citizen

00:34:02.300 --> 00:34:02.820 align:middle line:90%
discount.

00:34:02.820 --> 00:34:05.270 align:middle line:84%
Someone who doesn't even
really want to go to the play

00:34:05.270 --> 00:34:07.721 align:middle line:84%
is there and paying
because it's only $15.

00:34:07.721 --> 00:34:09.679 align:middle line:84%
And it was cheaper than
going to the movie they

00:34:09.679 --> 00:34:10.802 align:middle line:90%
would have rather seen.

00:34:10.802 --> 00:34:12.260 align:middle line:84%
And then, you have
someone else who

00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:14.552 align:middle line:84%
would have liked to go who
would be willing to pay $60,

00:34:14.552 --> 00:34:15.300 align:middle line:90%
but isn't there.

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:16.739 align:middle line:90%
And there would be this--

00:34:16.739 --> 00:34:18.739 align:middle line:84%
you could just make this
swap of, I'll trade you

00:34:18.739 --> 00:34:20.090 align:middle line:90%
my play ticket.

00:34:20.090 --> 00:34:22.280 align:middle line:84%
And I'll give you a
ticket to the movie

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:24.080 align:middle line:90%
in the theater next door.

00:34:24.080 --> 00:34:26.480 align:middle line:90%
We're both better off.

00:34:26.480 --> 00:34:31.300 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so there's that
misallocation of goods utility.

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:34.760 align:middle line:84%
A proposition just
highlighting the second effect

00:34:34.760 --> 00:34:38.389 align:middle line:84%
is, if third degree
discrimination doesn't increase

00:34:38.389 --> 00:34:41.120 align:middle line:84%
total output relative
to uniform pricing,

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:43.187 align:middle line:90%
then it reduces social welfare.

00:34:43.187 --> 00:34:45.770 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to be because
if it doesn't increase output,

00:34:45.770 --> 00:34:48.060 align:middle line:84%
it's not helping
with deadweight loss.

00:34:48.060 --> 00:34:50.210 align:middle line:84%
And so, if it's not helping
with deadweight loss,

00:34:50.210 --> 00:34:51.332 align:middle line:90%
it creates misallocation.

00:34:51.332 --> 00:34:54.199 align:middle line:84%
And therefore,
people are worse off.

00:34:54.199 --> 00:34:56.920 align:middle line:90%
So here's a proof.

00:34:56.920 --> 00:35:00.880 align:middle line:84%
So imagine, we start
from a situation

00:35:00.880 --> 00:35:03.160 align:middle line:90%
where we have uniform pricing.

00:35:03.160 --> 00:35:08.860 align:middle line:84%
And then we switch to
discriminatory pricing.

00:35:08.860 --> 00:35:13.570 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to write
delta x1 and delta x2

00:35:13.570 --> 00:35:17.620 align:middle line:90%
for how does demand change.

00:35:17.620 --> 00:35:20.935 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
write delta xi to be

00:35:20.935 --> 00:35:29.110 align:middle line:84%
xi under discriminatory
pricing, minus--

00:35:29.110 --> 00:35:31.900 align:middle line:84%
under discriminatory
pricing, minus the xi

00:35:31.900 --> 00:35:33.045 align:middle line:90%
under uniform pricing.

00:35:33.045 --> 00:35:36.200 align:middle line:90%


00:35:36.200 --> 00:35:37.607 align:middle line:90%
So here's a picture here.

00:35:37.607 --> 00:35:39.440 align:middle line:84%
I guess I'll use this
one instead of drawing

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:40.550 align:middle line:90%
a new one on the board.

00:35:40.550 --> 00:35:46.190 align:middle line:84%
So if we had uniform pricing,
this was going to be the price,

00:35:46.190 --> 00:35:47.340 align:middle line:90%
p*.

00:35:47.340 --> 00:35:50.540 align:middle line:84%
This would be the number
of units sold, x*.

00:35:50.540 --> 00:35:52.920 align:middle line:84%
And then, so this is
the student market.

00:35:52.920 --> 00:35:56.120 align:middle line:84%
These are the lower
value consumers.

00:35:56.120 --> 00:35:58.850 align:middle line:84%
When you start discriminating,
you give them a price cut.

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:01.740 align:middle line:84%
You cut their
price from p to p*.

00:36:01.740 --> 00:36:03.620 align:middle line:90%
We get a positive delta xi.

00:36:03.620 --> 00:36:06.190 align:middle line:84%
They buy this many units
instead of this many units.

00:36:06.190 --> 00:36:10.010 align:middle line:90%


00:36:10.010 --> 00:36:12.170 align:middle line:84%
So a comment is,
if I think about,

00:36:12.170 --> 00:36:14.550 align:middle line:84%
what is the welfare
gain in this market,

00:36:14.550 --> 00:36:16.010 align:middle line:84%
the welfare gain
in this market is,

00:36:16.010 --> 00:36:19.010 align:middle line:84%
I've reduced deadweight
loss by this much,

00:36:19.010 --> 00:36:24.790 align:middle line:84%
this trapezoid here, because
all of these units that were not

00:36:24.790 --> 00:36:26.540 align:middle line:84%
formally getting
purchased are now getting

00:36:26.540 --> 00:36:28.470 align:middle line:90%
purchased at the lower price.

00:36:28.470 --> 00:36:30.710 align:middle line:84%
And then this is the
amount of surplus

00:36:30.710 --> 00:36:34.250 align:middle line:84%
that's being generated on
each of those units that's

00:36:34.250 --> 00:36:35.460 align:middle line:90%
being purchased.

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:38.480 align:middle line:84%
So what I get is, if you
think about this trapezoid,

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:41.090 align:middle line:84%
it's bounded by
the big rectangle

00:36:41.090 --> 00:36:42.360 align:middle line:90%
and the little rectangle.

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:47.660 align:middle line:84%
And so that tells me that
p* minus c times delta xi,

00:36:47.660 --> 00:36:50.990 align:middle line:84%
that's the big rectangle,
is bigger than delta Wi,

00:36:50.990 --> 00:36:54.930 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than pi*
minus c, times delta xi.

00:36:54.930 --> 00:36:59.900 align:middle line:84%
So pi* minus c is the area
of this little rectangle.

00:36:59.900 --> 00:37:02.090 align:middle line:84%
That doesn't quite line
up, but underneath.

00:37:02.090 --> 00:37:05.070 align:middle line:84%
And then p* minus c is the
height of the big rectangle.

00:37:05.070 --> 00:37:08.210 align:middle line:84%
So the change in welfare is
bounded between those two.

00:37:08.210 --> 00:37:10.230 align:middle line:90%
Notice, the same is true--

00:37:10.230 --> 00:37:15.390 align:middle line:84%
if there's another market where
I reduce, I raise the price

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:20.520 align:middle line:84%
and I reduce the
demand, again, the loss

00:37:20.520 --> 00:37:23.400 align:middle line:84%
is going to be this
trapezoid over here.

00:37:23.400 --> 00:37:26.340 align:middle line:84%
And the loss with that trapezoid
is going to be bounded between

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:30.630 align:middle line:84%
two rectangles, one being the p*
minus c, which is now negative,

00:37:30.630 --> 00:37:33.340 align:middle line:84%
the other being the pi*
minus c, which is here.

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:35.880 align:middle line:84%
And with the negative
numbers, the same inequality

00:37:35.880 --> 00:37:36.605 align:middle line:90%
is still true.

00:37:36.605 --> 00:37:39.240 align:middle line:90%


00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:41.980 align:middle line:84%
It's now, pi* minus c is
the big negative number.

00:37:41.980 --> 00:37:43.510 align:middle line:84%
This is the small
negative number.

00:37:43.510 --> 00:37:45.420 align:middle line:84%
It is in between the
big negative number

00:37:45.420 --> 00:37:48.120 align:middle line:90%
and the small negative number.

00:37:48.120 --> 00:37:55.850 align:middle line:84%
So if I just now add that
across markets, summing over i,

00:37:55.850 --> 00:37:57.290 align:middle line:90%
I sum this over markets, i.

00:37:57.290 --> 00:38:01.710 align:middle line:84%
I get p* minus c times the
sum of delta xi is bigger than

00:38:01.710 --> 00:38:04.260 align:middle line:84%
the change in welfare, which
is bigger than the sum over i

00:38:04.260 --> 00:38:07.140 align:middle line:90%
of pi* minus c delta xi.

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:10.200 align:middle line:84%
And the left one is the
one that's really involved

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:11.970 align:middle line:90%
in proving the proposition.

00:38:11.970 --> 00:38:16.380 align:middle line:84%
If the sum of the delta xi*, if
the sum of the delta xi's is 0

00:38:16.380 --> 00:38:20.430 align:middle line:84%
or negative, then this is
0 or a negative number.

00:38:20.430 --> 00:38:22.920 align:middle line:84%
And it's bigger than
the change in welfare.

00:38:22.920 --> 00:38:25.200 align:middle line:84%
So the change in
welfare is negative

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:26.795 align:middle line:90%
if output hasn't gone up.

00:38:26.795 --> 00:38:36.360 align:middle line:90%


00:38:36.360 --> 00:38:39.050 align:middle line:90%
OK, so some comments on here.

00:38:39.050 --> 00:38:43.080 align:middle line:90%


00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:44.443 align:middle line:90%
This is a result about welfare.

00:38:44.443 --> 00:38:46.860 align:middle line:84%
It's highlighting that there's
that misallocation of goods

00:38:46.860 --> 00:38:47.710 align:middle line:90%
sold.

00:38:47.710 --> 00:38:50.200 align:middle line:90%
Obviously, output can go up.

00:38:50.200 --> 00:38:53.130 align:middle line:84%
If output goes up, and
output goes up by enough,

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:58.230 align:middle line:84%
then welfare can be higher
with price discrimination.

00:38:58.230 --> 00:39:02.100 align:middle line:84%
One example of that
would be in the model

00:39:02.100 --> 00:39:06.072 align:middle line:84%
where you only serve one market
when you're discriminating.

00:39:06.072 --> 00:39:07.530 align:middle line:84%
Then, obviously,
output has gone up

00:39:07.530 --> 00:39:10.980 align:middle line:84%
by the amount at which the
students are buying things.

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:14.250 align:middle line:84%
Equity concerns
can go either way.

00:39:14.250 --> 00:39:16.360 align:middle line:84%
We think about--
price discrimination,

00:39:16.360 --> 00:39:19.350 align:middle line:84%
also, I said, is a
transfer between groups,

00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:22.480 align:middle line:84%
between the high demand group
to the low demand group.

00:39:22.480 --> 00:39:28.450 align:middle line:84%
What we think about that
depends on the examples.

00:39:28.450 --> 00:39:30.240 align:middle line:84%
So for instance,
one case where we

00:39:30.240 --> 00:39:32.610 align:middle line:84%
might think equity says
we should discriminate

00:39:32.610 --> 00:39:34.900 align:middle line:84%
is financial aid for
undergraduate colleges

00:39:34.900 --> 00:39:36.550 align:middle line:90%
in the US.

00:39:36.550 --> 00:39:38.553 align:middle line:84%
Colleges charge very,
very different prices

00:39:38.553 --> 00:39:39.470 align:middle line:90%
to different students.

00:39:39.470 --> 00:39:41.530 align:middle line:84%
They charge much less
to low income students

00:39:41.530 --> 00:39:43.490 align:middle line:84%
than they charge to
high income students.

00:39:43.490 --> 00:39:46.210 align:middle line:84%
We tend to think of banning
price discrimination there would

00:39:46.210 --> 00:39:49.630 align:middle line:84%
be a bad thing because it would
be raising the prices paid

00:39:49.630 --> 00:39:55.060 align:middle line:84%
by all the low income students,
who perhaps have a higher

00:39:55.060 --> 00:39:56.375 align:middle line:90%
marginal utility of income.

00:39:56.375 --> 00:39:58.750 align:middle line:84%
Another example, though, that
goes the opposite direction

00:39:58.750 --> 00:39:59.780 align:middle line:90%
is health insurance.

00:39:59.780 --> 00:40:02.770 align:middle line:84%
If you allow health insurance
companies to discriminate,

00:40:02.770 --> 00:40:05.480 align:middle line:84%
the people who should pay the
high price are the sick people.

00:40:05.480 --> 00:40:09.850 align:middle line:84%
And so, if you ban
discrimination--

00:40:09.850 --> 00:40:12.160 align:middle line:84%
if you allow discrimination
for health insurance,

00:40:12.160 --> 00:40:17.830 align:middle line:84%
there, allowing discrimination
will be charging high prices

00:40:17.830 --> 00:40:18.650 align:middle line:90%
to sick people.

00:40:18.650 --> 00:40:19.750 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we don't want
to allow you to charge

00:40:19.750 --> 00:40:21.667 align:middle line:84%
high prices to sick
people for equity reasons.

00:40:21.667 --> 00:40:25.970 align:middle line:90%


00:40:25.970 --> 00:40:32.120 align:middle line:84%
OK, here is a corollary of
the previous theorem, which

00:40:32.120 --> 00:40:35.210 align:middle line:90%
I think is important to know.

00:40:35.210 --> 00:40:40.400 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that demands in all
these sub-markets are linear

00:40:40.400 --> 00:40:43.870 align:middle line:84%
and that all markets are
served under uniform pricing.

00:40:43.870 --> 00:40:45.890 align:middle line:84%
Then third degree
price discrimination

00:40:45.890 --> 00:40:48.320 align:middle line:90%
always reduces welfare.

00:40:48.320 --> 00:40:52.160 align:middle line:84%
So, again, why demand should
be linear, I don't know.

00:40:52.160 --> 00:40:53.660 align:middle line:84%
But it's a common
benchmark that you

00:40:53.660 --> 00:40:56.210 align:middle line:84%
might use as your first thought
if you're studying a market.

00:40:56.210 --> 00:41:01.340 align:middle line:84%
And this theorem says that, if
the demands are linear, then

00:41:01.340 --> 00:41:02.780 align:middle line:84%
third degree price
discrimination

00:41:02.780 --> 00:41:05.780 align:middle line:90%
reduces social welfare.

00:41:05.780 --> 00:41:07.140 align:middle line:90%
What's the argument for that?

00:41:07.140 --> 00:41:11.210 align:middle line:84%
The argument for this is
going to be that the sum--

00:41:11.210 --> 00:41:15.350 align:middle line:84%
in the linear model, the sum
of the delta xi's is always

00:41:15.350 --> 00:41:16.760 align:middle line:90%
equal to 0.

00:41:16.760 --> 00:41:20.090 align:middle line:84%
And because the
output is unchanged,

00:41:20.090 --> 00:41:24.050 align:middle line:84%
then discrimination
hurts social welfare.

00:41:24.050 --> 00:41:26.760 align:middle line:90%
How do I show that?

00:41:26.760 --> 00:41:28.840 align:middle line:84%
This is algebra, so I
won't go through it.

00:41:28.840 --> 00:41:32.675 align:middle line:84%
But this was something
I think I've shown you

00:41:32.675 --> 00:41:34.800 align:middle line:84%
over and over again when
I've been drawing pictures

00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:35.830 align:middle line:90%
on the board.

00:41:35.830 --> 00:41:37.865 align:middle line:84%
Suppose you have a
linear demand curve.

00:41:37.865 --> 00:41:40.530 align:middle line:90%


00:41:40.530 --> 00:41:45.420 align:middle line:90%
So this is my x of p.

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:47.490 align:middle line:90%
And this is p on this axis.

00:41:47.490 --> 00:41:51.060 align:middle line:90%
I draw my cost in here.

00:41:51.060 --> 00:41:55.470 align:middle line:84%
And then I ask, what's the
monopoly price when I set--

00:41:55.470 --> 00:41:58.530 align:middle line:84%
what's the monopoly price
when I have cost of c

00:41:58.530 --> 00:42:00.360 align:middle line:84%
and I have a linear
demand curve?

00:42:00.360 --> 00:42:02.820 align:middle line:90%
It's exactly in the middle.

00:42:02.820 --> 00:42:06.900 align:middle line:84%
So the rectangle
that maximizes this

00:42:06.900 --> 00:42:14.190 align:middle line:84%
is to set p halfway in between
the price that's competitive.

00:42:14.190 --> 00:42:18.120 align:middle line:84%
Oops, what am I
doing wrong here?

00:42:18.120 --> 00:42:20.670 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, that's not what I wanted.

00:42:20.670 --> 00:42:23.220 align:middle line:90%
I wanted x here and p here.

00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:30.060 align:middle line:84%
So if I set price equal
to cost, then this

00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:33.330 align:middle line:84%
is the x in a
competitive market.

00:42:33.330 --> 00:42:37.470 align:middle line:90%
This is the 0.

00:42:37.470 --> 00:42:42.660 align:middle line:84%
x monopoly equals 1/2
[INAUDIBLE] competitive.

00:42:42.660 --> 00:42:45.220 align:middle line:84%
And then this is the
monopoly price here.

00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:47.993 align:middle line:84%
So if I put this
on the x axis, I'm

00:42:47.993 --> 00:42:49.660 align:middle line:84%
trying to figure out
the monopoly price.

00:42:49.660 --> 00:42:54.690 align:middle line:84%
It's always halfway in between
0 and what a competitive firm

00:42:54.690 --> 00:42:55.500 align:middle line:90%
would charge--

00:42:55.500 --> 00:42:59.550 align:middle line:90%
would produce, OK?

00:42:59.550 --> 00:43:07.270 align:middle line:84%
So xi* is just one half
of the competitive output.

00:43:07.270 --> 00:43:12.750 align:middle line:84%
So if you sum across
markets, what you just find

00:43:12.750 --> 00:43:19.020 align:middle line:84%
is that the sum of the
xi's is just one half

00:43:19.020 --> 00:43:21.600 align:middle line:84%
of the sum of what you'd get
under perfect competition

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:23.650 align:middle line:90%
in each market together.

00:43:23.650 --> 00:43:25.403 align:middle line:84%
And if you think about
perfect competition

00:43:25.403 --> 00:43:26.820 align:middle line:84%
with all the markets
combined, you

00:43:26.820 --> 00:43:29.145 align:middle line:84%
set p equal to c,
that's still one

00:43:29.145 --> 00:43:31.020 align:middle line:84%
half of what you get
with perfect competition

00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:32.550 align:middle line:90%
in all markets.

00:43:32.550 --> 00:43:35.940 align:middle line:84%
So if I combine the
markets together,

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:38.820 align:middle line:84%
I get one half of the
competitive market

00:43:38.820 --> 00:43:40.300 align:middle line:90%
as the total output.

00:43:40.300 --> 00:43:41.880 align:middle line:84%
And if I separate
the markets, I get

00:43:41.880 --> 00:43:44.100 align:middle line:84%
one half of the competitive
output in market 1,

00:43:44.100 --> 00:43:46.560 align:middle line:84%
plus one half of the
competitive output in market 2,

00:43:46.560 --> 00:43:49.380 align:middle line:84%
plus one half of the
competitive output in market n.

00:43:49.380 --> 00:43:51.810 align:middle line:84%
That's just-- because
the competitive output

00:43:51.810 --> 00:43:54.690 align:middle line:84%
is always the demand
at price equal to c,

00:43:54.690 --> 00:43:57.350 align:middle line:90%
those two things are equal.

00:43:57.350 --> 00:43:59.790 align:middle line:84%
So the sum of one half of
the competitive outputs,

00:43:59.790 --> 00:44:02.880 align:middle line:84%
market by market, is one half
of the competitive output

00:44:02.880 --> 00:44:05.130 align:middle line:90%
in all the markets together.

00:44:05.130 --> 00:44:10.620 align:middle line:84%
So with linear demand curves,
because of this feature

00:44:10.620 --> 00:44:15.630 align:middle line:84%
that you sell exactly half
the competitive output,

00:44:15.630 --> 00:44:17.955 align:middle line:90%
output is unchanged under--

00:44:17.955 --> 00:44:21.100 align:middle line:90%


00:44:21.100 --> 00:44:23.100 align:middle line:84%
output is unchanged if
you allow discrimination.

00:44:23.100 --> 00:44:26.730 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, discrimination
reduces social welfare, OK?

00:44:26.730 --> 00:44:29.480 align:middle line:90%


00:44:29.480 --> 00:44:31.460 align:middle line:84%
I covered this theorem
because I think

00:44:31.460 --> 00:44:35.750 align:middle line:84%
it highlights a very general
concern, which is, often,

00:44:35.750 --> 00:44:38.270 align:middle line:84%
people, when they're doing
structural estimation of models,

00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:40.790 align:middle line:84%
they assume some
theoretical structure,

00:44:40.790 --> 00:44:43.430 align:middle line:84%
whether on the demand curves
or on the utility functions.

00:44:43.430 --> 00:44:45.650 align:middle line:84%
And then they estimate
some number of parameters.

00:44:45.650 --> 00:44:47.990 align:middle line:84%
And then they comment on
something of interest.

00:44:47.990 --> 00:44:49.917 align:middle line:90%
So this gives you an example.

00:44:49.917 --> 00:44:51.500 align:middle line:84%
Suppose someone comes
to you and says,

00:44:51.500 --> 00:44:53.070 align:middle line:90%
OK, I'm going to write a paper.

00:44:53.070 --> 00:44:55.790 align:middle line:84%
I'm concerned about
price discrimination

00:44:55.790 --> 00:44:58.135 align:middle line:90%
in health insurance markets.

00:44:58.135 --> 00:45:00.260 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to imagine that,
instead of being allowed

00:45:00.260 --> 00:45:03.530 align:middle line:84%
to discriminate and charge
different health-related prices,

00:45:03.530 --> 00:45:06.237 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to charge equal
prices in all market.

00:45:06.237 --> 00:45:08.570 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to see what's
the effect on social welfare

00:45:08.570 --> 00:45:12.170 align:middle line:84%
of forcing the firm to charge a
uniform price across all classes

00:45:12.170 --> 00:45:14.990 align:middle line:84%
of consumers instead of
discriminating by consumers.

00:45:14.990 --> 00:45:17.630 align:middle line:84%
And the student tells me,
oh, wait, for simplicity,

00:45:17.630 --> 00:45:19.380 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to assume
that demand curves are

00:45:19.380 --> 00:45:20.780 align:middle line:90%
linear in every market.

00:45:20.780 --> 00:45:23.868 align:middle line:84%
Well, then, I already know what
the results of the paper are.

00:45:23.868 --> 00:45:25.410 align:middle line:84%
I know that the
paper is going to say

00:45:25.410 --> 00:45:28.890 align:middle line:84%
that charging uniform price
has increased social welfare.

00:45:28.890 --> 00:45:30.360 align:middle line:84%
And it's increased
social welfare

00:45:30.360 --> 00:45:33.630 align:middle line:84%
because it's implicit in
this demand specification

00:45:33.630 --> 00:45:41.310 align:middle line:84%
that you increase social welfare
when you ban discrimination.

00:45:41.310 --> 00:45:44.430 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's just a very
important thing to know,

00:45:44.430 --> 00:45:47.225 align:middle line:84%
that if you're thinking
about writing about a model

00:45:47.225 --> 00:45:48.600 align:middle line:84%
to investigate
some question, you

00:45:48.600 --> 00:45:50.350 align:middle line:84%
have to know whether
the answer is already

00:45:50.350 --> 00:45:53.340 align:middle line:84%
determined by this functional
form you've chosen.

00:45:53.340 --> 00:45:57.570 align:middle line:84%
And again, why is linear
demand curves not right?

00:45:57.570 --> 00:46:02.160 align:middle line:84%
In part, social welfare is
about how much utility people

00:46:02.160 --> 00:46:04.150 align:middle line:90%
are getting beyond--

00:46:04.150 --> 00:46:08.130 align:middle line:84%
what's the consumer surplus
that people are getting?

00:46:08.130 --> 00:46:11.455 align:middle line:84%
It gets very different if
demand looks like this,

00:46:11.455 --> 00:46:13.830 align:middle line:84%
and if there are some people
who really like the product,

00:46:13.830 --> 00:46:15.300 align:middle line:84%
or if it goes like
that and there's

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:18.060 align:middle line:90%
nobody who likes it very much.

00:46:18.060 --> 00:46:21.180 align:middle line:84%
In some sense,
prices are determined

00:46:21.180 --> 00:46:22.650 align:middle line:84%
by elasticities,
which are just--

00:46:22.650 --> 00:46:24.062 align:middle line:90%
determine these slopes here.

00:46:24.062 --> 00:46:26.020 align:middle line:84%
If you actually want to
understand whether it's

00:46:26.020 --> 00:46:27.790 align:middle line:84%
increasing or
decreasing welfare,

00:46:27.790 --> 00:46:29.650 align:middle line:84%
you'd have to be
able to think about,

00:46:29.650 --> 00:46:31.840 align:middle line:90%
can I estimate these things?

00:46:31.840 --> 00:46:34.540 align:middle line:84%
How much consumer
surplus is here,

00:46:34.540 --> 00:46:36.777 align:middle line:84%
relative to what
the monopolist does,

00:46:36.777 --> 00:46:38.860 align:middle line:84%
which is based on the
elasticity at this one point

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:40.930 align:middle line:90%
the monopolist is choosing.

00:46:40.930 --> 00:46:47.590 align:middle line:84%
And you need to be aware
that this assumption is

00:46:47.590 --> 00:46:51.010 align:middle line:90%
picking the answer for you.

00:46:51.010 --> 00:46:53.980 align:middle line:90%
I don't know that it's not--

00:46:53.980 --> 00:46:56.395 align:middle line:84%
it may be a useful rule of
thumb just to keep in mind.

00:46:56.395 --> 00:46:59.020 align:middle line:84%
If, in the linear model, there's
no effect on aggregate output,

00:46:59.020 --> 00:47:00.770 align:middle line:84%
maybe we would think
that aggregate output

00:47:00.770 --> 00:47:02.530 align:middle line:90%
effects typically are small.

00:47:02.530 --> 00:47:05.595 align:middle line:84%
But again, it's all based
in the linearity there.

00:47:05.595 --> 00:47:08.840 align:middle line:90%


00:47:08.840 --> 00:47:10.910 align:middle line:90%
OK, what is general?

00:47:10.910 --> 00:47:15.320 align:middle line:84%
So there's a recent paper by
Bergemann, Brooks, and Morris

00:47:15.320 --> 00:47:21.080 align:middle line:84%
that asked this question,
what can we say generally

00:47:21.080 --> 00:47:23.930 align:middle line:84%
about when you allow a
monopolist to discriminate?

00:47:23.930 --> 00:47:26.180 align:middle line:84%
Will welfare-- we
know that, you allow

00:47:26.180 --> 00:47:28.040 align:middle line:84%
monopolist to discriminate,
profits go up.

00:47:28.040 --> 00:47:29.940 align:middle line:90%
Will social welfare go up?

00:47:29.940 --> 00:47:32.140 align:middle line:84%
Will consumer surplus
go up or down?

00:47:32.140 --> 00:47:33.680 align:middle line:90%
What are the trade-offs?

00:47:33.680 --> 00:47:35.840 align:middle line:84%
And what Bergemann,
Brooks, and Morris note

00:47:35.840 --> 00:47:39.500 align:middle line:84%
is that it depends
on what information

00:47:39.500 --> 00:47:41.910 align:middle line:84%
the monopolist has
about the consumers.

00:47:41.910 --> 00:47:44.210 align:middle line:84%
So let's think about
the standard example

00:47:44.210 --> 00:47:49.820 align:middle line:84%
where the consumer's values
are uniform, 0, 1, c equals 0.

00:47:49.820 --> 00:47:53.750 align:middle line:84%
If the monopolist has no
information about the consumers,

00:47:53.750 --> 00:47:56.142 align:middle line:84%
then it doesn't matter if you
allow him to discriminate.

00:47:56.142 --> 00:47:58.100 align:middle line:84%
He has no information on
which to discriminate.

00:47:58.100 --> 00:48:00.220 align:middle line:84%
What's the monopolist
going to do?

00:48:00.220 --> 00:48:04.250 align:middle line:84%
This is your standard monopoly
pricing, p equals 1/2.

00:48:04.250 --> 00:48:05.610 align:middle line:90%
Profit is 1/4.

00:48:05.610 --> 00:48:08.700 align:middle line:90%
Consumer surplus is 1/8.

00:48:08.700 --> 00:48:10.890 align:middle line:84%
In their figure,
that's this point here.

00:48:10.890 --> 00:48:13.170 align:middle line:90%
Consumer surplus is 1/8.

00:48:13.170 --> 00:48:17.880 align:middle line:90%
Producer surplus is 1/4, OK?

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:20.700 align:middle line:90%


00:48:20.700 --> 00:48:23.850 align:middle line:84%
One extreme is, what
if the monopolist

00:48:23.850 --> 00:48:26.200 align:middle line:84%
can perform first degree
price discrimination?

00:48:26.200 --> 00:48:28.350 align:middle line:90%
The monopolist knows everything.

00:48:28.350 --> 00:48:30.690 align:middle line:84%
If the monopolist
knows everything,

00:48:30.690 --> 00:48:35.140 align:middle line:84%
then the monopolist charges
each consumer their full value.

00:48:35.140 --> 00:48:37.800 align:middle line:84%
We know there that the
monopolist's profit

00:48:37.800 --> 00:48:41.490 align:middle line:90%
has gone up tremendously.

00:48:41.490 --> 00:48:44.630 align:middle line:84%
And we know that the consumer
surplus has been driven to 0.

00:48:44.630 --> 00:48:48.870 align:middle line:84%
So here, if the monopolist knows
everything about the consumers,

00:48:48.870 --> 00:48:52.260 align:middle line:84%
then the answer to what happens
if you allow discrimination is,

00:48:52.260 --> 00:48:54.420 align:middle line:90%
profit goes up--

00:48:54.420 --> 00:48:55.740 align:middle line:90%
profit goes up.

00:48:55.740 --> 00:48:57.480 align:middle line:90%
Consumer surplus goes down.

00:48:57.480 --> 00:49:00.830 align:middle line:84%
Social welfare goes up because
we're above the 45 degree line.

00:49:00.830 --> 00:49:04.980 align:middle line:90%


00:49:04.980 --> 00:49:07.350 align:middle line:84%
But they note, in
the paper, thinking

00:49:07.350 --> 00:49:09.840 align:middle line:84%
the same kind of reasoning
as Roesler and Szentes,

00:49:09.840 --> 00:49:15.510 align:middle line:84%
there are other situations where
consumer surplus and profit will

00:49:15.510 --> 00:49:19.150 align:middle line:84%
also go up if you allow the
monopolist to discriminate,

00:49:19.150 --> 00:49:21.140 align:middle line:90%
that they both go up.

00:49:21.140 --> 00:49:24.590 align:middle line:84%
Here's their interesting
and clever example.

00:49:24.590 --> 00:49:28.450 align:middle line:84%
So suppose what we have is,
we have this demand curve that

00:49:28.450 --> 00:49:33.180 align:middle line:84%
comes from there being
consumers with every different--

00:49:33.180 --> 00:49:35.860 align:middle line:84%
these sub-populations
of consumers

00:49:35.860 --> 00:49:38.080 align:middle line:90%
with every different value.

00:49:38.080 --> 00:49:41.380 align:middle line:84%
Suppose what the monopolist
gets is this signal that says,

00:49:41.380 --> 00:49:43.420 align:middle line:90%
one of two things is true.

00:49:43.420 --> 00:49:51.220 align:middle line:84%
Either the consumer is
someone whose value--

00:49:51.220 --> 00:49:56.620 align:middle line:84%
so this is going to be my
f of v, given my signal.

00:49:56.620 --> 00:50:02.380 align:middle line:84%
The consumer either has a
value between 0.01 and 0.02,

00:50:02.380 --> 00:50:06.160 align:middle line:84%
or the consumer's value
is, with small probability,

00:50:06.160 --> 00:50:11.080 align:middle line:84%
drawn from a uniform
distribution on 0.02 up to 1.

00:50:11.080 --> 00:50:14.170 align:middle line:84%
So this signal means there's
no way their value is

00:50:14.170 --> 00:50:15.950 align:middle line:90%
between 0 and 0.01.

00:50:15.950 --> 00:50:18.360 align:middle line:90%
Very likely, it's between--

00:50:18.360 --> 00:50:20.070 align:middle line:84%
all consumers with
values in this range

00:50:20.070 --> 00:50:21.400 align:middle line:90%
will have that signal.

00:50:21.400 --> 00:50:24.580 align:middle line:84%
And then there's some small
possibility of a false positive.

00:50:24.580 --> 00:50:27.450 align:middle line:84%
And it's actually a consumer
with a value drawn from here,

00:50:27.450 --> 00:50:29.400 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:50:29.400 --> 00:50:32.730 align:middle line:90%
What does your profit look like?

00:50:32.730 --> 00:50:45.460 align:middle line:84%
Well, your profit is increasing
in price until you get to 0.01.

00:50:45.460 --> 00:50:52.720 align:middle line:84%
As you drop your price from 0.01
to 0.02, per consumer profit,

00:50:52.720 --> 00:50:54.866 align:middle line:90%
you'd lose many customers.

00:50:54.866 --> 00:50:58.560 align:middle line:90%


00:50:58.560 --> 00:51:01.920 align:middle line:84%
But then, once you
raise your price,

00:51:01.920 --> 00:51:03.433 align:middle line:84%
you then are just
making more money.

00:51:03.433 --> 00:51:05.100 align:middle line:84%
Now, all these consumers
have gone away.

00:51:05.100 --> 00:51:06.780 align:middle line:84%
And you're making more
money off these consumers

00:51:06.780 --> 00:51:08.010 align:middle line:90%
as you raise your price.

00:51:08.010 --> 00:51:11.580 align:middle line:84%
And you have something
that looks like this.

00:51:11.580 --> 00:51:14.040 align:middle line:84%
In this sub-population, it's
like your standard monopoly

00:51:14.040 --> 00:51:16.350 align:middle line:90%
pricing.

00:51:16.350 --> 00:51:20.280 align:middle line:84%
And what they note is that,
by very clever choice of how

00:51:20.280 --> 00:51:23.220 align:middle line:84%
many people to put
in this band, I

00:51:23.220 --> 00:51:28.620 align:middle line:84%
can make it so that this profit
level and this profit level

00:51:28.620 --> 00:51:30.900 align:middle line:90%
coincide.

00:51:30.900 --> 00:51:35.670 align:middle line:84%
So I'm exactly indifferent
between two prices, 0.01,

00:51:35.670 --> 00:51:37.610 align:middle line:90%
or my standard 0.5 price.

00:51:37.610 --> 00:51:40.340 align:middle line:90%


00:51:40.340 --> 00:51:43.280 align:middle line:84%
And so, if you give the
monopolist a signal that says,

00:51:43.280 --> 00:51:45.365 align:middle line:84%
there's a few
consumers with this--

00:51:45.365 --> 00:51:47.900 align:middle line:90%


00:51:47.900 --> 00:51:48.930 align:middle line:90%
here's this signal.

00:51:48.930 --> 00:51:51.950 align:middle line:84%
You get this signal, or you
get this signal occasionally.

00:51:51.950 --> 00:51:54.617 align:middle line:84%
And then, the rest of the time,
you just get the regular signal.

00:51:54.617 --> 00:51:55.790 align:middle line:90%
It's a regular customer.

00:51:55.790 --> 00:52:00.253 align:middle line:90%
Then, when you get this signal--

00:52:00.253 --> 00:52:02.420 align:middle line:84%
and then, you have the other
signal that tells you--

00:52:02.420 --> 00:52:04.070 align:middle line:84%
the complementary
signal would tell me

00:52:04.070 --> 00:52:10.130 align:middle line:84%
that the consumer might
have a value here, no chance

00:52:10.130 --> 00:52:11.090 align:middle line:90%
to have a value here.

00:52:11.090 --> 00:52:13.583 align:middle line:84%
And then they may have
a value like that.

00:52:13.583 --> 00:52:15.000 align:middle line:84%
So that's my
complementary signal.

00:52:15.000 --> 00:52:18.380 align:middle line:84%
It's either this signal
or this signal, OK?

00:52:18.380 --> 00:52:24.980 align:middle line:84%
Well, when I get this signal, my
optimal price is p equals 0.5.

00:52:24.980 --> 00:52:27.660 align:middle line:84%
When I get this signal, I'm
indifferent between 0.01

00:52:27.660 --> 00:52:28.830 align:middle line:90%
and 0.5.

00:52:28.830 --> 00:52:32.390 align:middle line:84%
So if I charge 0.01
when I get this signal,

00:52:32.390 --> 00:52:35.270 align:middle line:84%
I've just created
consumer surplus.

00:52:35.270 --> 00:52:37.730 align:middle line:84%
And I haven't affected
profits at all.

00:52:37.730 --> 00:52:40.980 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm able to do
is move along this line

00:52:40.980 --> 00:52:44.680 align:middle line:84%
and just keep profits
exactly at the same thing

00:52:44.680 --> 00:52:49.503 align:middle line:84%
I get charging 0.5 to every
one, and give consumer surplus.

00:52:49.503 --> 00:52:50.920 align:middle line:84%
And then you can
think about this.

00:52:50.920 --> 00:52:52.020 align:middle line:84%
I could do the same
argument, adding

00:52:52.020 --> 00:52:53.290 align:middle line:90%
more and more such signals.

00:52:53.290 --> 00:52:58.660 align:middle line:84%
I can do a 0.02 to 0.03 signal,
a 0.03 to 0.04, 0.04 to 0.05.

00:52:58.660 --> 00:53:01.740 align:middle line:84%
I can give the monopolist
many, many, many signals,

00:53:01.740 --> 00:53:04.200 align:middle line:84%
and just, by doing
that, get the monopolist

00:53:04.200 --> 00:53:07.680 align:middle line:84%
to have more and more groups
to which it offers a discount

00:53:07.680 --> 00:53:10.565 align:middle line:84%
and increase consumer surplus
up to some point here.

00:53:10.565 --> 00:53:18.830 align:middle line:90%


00:53:18.830 --> 00:53:20.850 align:middle line:90%
It turns out, I can also--

00:53:20.850 --> 00:53:23.390 align:middle line:84%
so that's their
clever construction

00:53:23.390 --> 00:53:25.595 align:middle line:84%
for how I get from here,
going in this direction.

00:53:25.595 --> 00:53:28.220 align:middle line:84%
It turns out I can also do the
same kind of clever construction

00:53:28.220 --> 00:53:29.660 align:middle line:90%
and go in that direction.

00:53:29.660 --> 00:53:32.270 align:middle line:84%
I can make the monopolist
indifferent between 0.5

00:53:32.270 --> 00:53:34.105 align:middle line:90%
and a very high price.

00:53:34.105 --> 00:53:36.230 align:middle line:84%
And then, once the monopolist
is indifferent to 0.5

00:53:36.230 --> 00:53:39.500 align:middle line:84%
and a very high price, they
start charging the high price

00:53:39.500 --> 00:53:40.730 align:middle line:90%
to all these.

00:53:40.730 --> 00:53:44.270 align:middle line:84%
There's a special signal
that tells you that.

00:53:44.270 --> 00:53:47.630 align:middle line:84%
There's some chance, there's
this excess probability

00:53:47.630 --> 00:53:49.130 align:middle line:90%
that the value is up here.

00:53:49.130 --> 00:53:50.820 align:middle line:90%
And otherwise, it's uniform.

00:53:50.820 --> 00:53:53.535 align:middle line:84%
When you get those
signals, they're

00:53:53.535 --> 00:53:55.160 align:middle line:84%
in between charging
the very high price

00:53:55.160 --> 00:53:59.930 align:middle line:84%
in selling to these consumers
only, or setting the 0.5 price.

00:53:59.930 --> 00:54:05.150 align:middle line:84%
And so, by using other signals,
I can keep the profit--

00:54:05.150 --> 00:54:08.670 align:middle line:84%
here, the profit went up as
consumer surplus went to 0.

00:54:08.670 --> 00:54:12.750 align:middle line:84%
Here, I can have the monopolist
profit stay the same and drive

00:54:12.750 --> 00:54:15.540 align:middle line:90%
consumer surplus to 0.

00:54:15.540 --> 00:54:18.330 align:middle line:84%
And their general result
is, by this choice

00:54:18.330 --> 00:54:20.920 align:middle line:84%
of clever distributions
of signals,

00:54:20.920 --> 00:54:27.480 align:middle line:84%
what they show is that, really,
what price discrimination does

00:54:27.480 --> 00:54:29.790 align:middle line:84%
to consumer surplus
and to social welfare

00:54:29.790 --> 00:54:32.250 align:middle line:90%
is just totally indeterminate.

00:54:32.250 --> 00:54:37.170 align:middle line:84%
If the monopolist has some
types of information structures,

00:54:37.170 --> 00:54:38.730 align:middle line:84%
it always raises
the monopoly profit.

00:54:38.730 --> 00:54:41.590 align:middle line:84%
But it can raise or
lower consumer surplus.

00:54:41.590 --> 00:54:44.130 align:middle line:84%
And because it can go above
or below this 45 degree line,

00:54:44.130 --> 00:54:47.220 align:middle line:84%
it can raise or lower
total social welfare.

00:54:47.220 --> 00:54:50.700 align:middle line:84%
And it's all a function
of, what information

00:54:50.700 --> 00:54:52.380 align:middle line:90%
does the monopolist have?

00:54:52.380 --> 00:54:53.970 align:middle line:84%
And is it the type
of information

00:54:53.970 --> 00:54:56.713 align:middle line:84%
that makes the monopolist
want to charge low prices,

00:54:56.713 --> 00:54:59.130 align:middle line:84%
or is it the type of information
that wants the monopolist

00:54:59.130 --> 00:55:00.335 align:middle line:90%
to charge high prices?

00:55:00.335 --> 00:55:04.600 align:middle line:90%


00:55:04.600 --> 00:55:06.370 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So this
result goes away

00:55:06.370 --> 00:55:09.580 align:middle line:84%
if the monopolist is allowed
to design their own information

00:55:09.580 --> 00:55:10.110 align:middle line:90%
scheme?

00:55:10.110 --> 00:55:11.277 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: That's right.

00:55:11.277 --> 00:55:14.770 align:middle line:84%
So this is very much thinking
about the-- the monopolist is

00:55:14.770 --> 00:55:18.130 align:middle line:84%
given a scheme that, it's
given a scheme like you either

00:55:18.130 --> 00:55:20.380 align:middle line:84%
know that the person
is astute, because it's

00:55:20.380 --> 00:55:22.630 align:middle line:90%
third degree discrimination.

00:55:22.630 --> 00:55:24.860 align:middle line:84%
You need to be able to
price based on the signals

00:55:24.860 --> 00:55:25.930 align:middle line:90%
that you're given.

00:55:25.930 --> 00:55:29.050 align:middle line:84%
You've got demand in
Mississippi, demand in Kansas,

00:55:29.050 --> 00:55:29.890 align:middle line:90%
demand in Boston.

00:55:29.890 --> 00:55:30.730 align:middle line:90%
That's all you know.

00:55:30.730 --> 00:55:32.710 align:middle line:90%
Or you've got student demand.

00:55:32.710 --> 00:55:37.120 align:middle line:90%
You've got non-student demand.

00:55:37.120 --> 00:55:42.130 align:middle line:84%
So, obviously, the monopolist
wants perfect information

00:55:42.130 --> 00:55:43.670 align:middle line:90%
and wants to go here.

00:55:43.670 --> 00:55:46.448 align:middle line:84%
And so, the more useful
information the monopolist has,

00:55:46.448 --> 00:55:47.990 align:middle line:84%
the higher it's
going to get up here.

00:55:47.990 --> 00:55:52.120 align:middle line:84%
But if the sub-populations
are what they are,

00:55:52.120 --> 00:55:54.550 align:middle line:90%
it can go either way, yeah.

00:55:54.550 --> 00:55:59.530 align:middle line:90%


00:55:59.530 --> 00:56:01.810 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, because I think,
on the monopolist's side,

00:56:01.810 --> 00:56:04.150 align:middle line:84%
the real intuition is, you
want to know everything

00:56:04.150 --> 00:56:05.210 align:middle line:90%
and do first degree.

00:56:05.210 --> 00:56:07.580 align:middle line:84%
And if you can do that, that's
what you're going to do.

00:56:07.580 --> 00:56:11.050 align:middle line:84%
But as you're
getting there, it's

00:56:11.050 --> 00:56:14.170 align:middle line:84%
possible you're going
there this way or this way

00:56:14.170 --> 00:56:15.170 align:middle line:90%
and reducing welfare.

00:56:15.170 --> 00:56:16.300 align:middle line:84%
It could be, you're
going there this way

00:56:16.300 --> 00:56:18.090 align:middle line:84%
and increasing welfare
on the path there.

00:56:18.090 --> 00:56:23.550 align:middle line:90%


00:56:23.550 --> 00:56:27.417 align:middle line:84%
OK, so the final thing
I'm going to do today,

00:56:27.417 --> 00:56:28.875 align:middle line:84%
well, actually,
next to last thing,

00:56:28.875 --> 00:56:31.080 align:middle line:90%
is second degree discrimination.

00:56:31.080 --> 00:56:36.480 align:middle line:84%
So now, we're back in the
case of the iPhone 128

00:56:36.480 --> 00:56:39.900 align:middle line:84%
and the iPhone 512, where
the monopolist can't observe

00:56:39.900 --> 00:56:43.140 align:middle line:84%
the consumer preferences and is
relying on the self-selection

00:56:43.140 --> 00:56:47.670 align:middle line:84%
to get the high value consumers
to buy the high-end good.

00:56:47.670 --> 00:56:51.900 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to have consumers
have some unknown type, theta,

00:56:51.900 --> 00:56:54.040 align:middle line:84%
that's unobserved
by the monopolist.

00:56:54.040 --> 00:57:00.300 align:middle line:84%
They get utility, v of q theta
if they buy a quality, q, good,

00:57:00.300 --> 00:57:01.560 align:middle line:90%
and their type is theta.

00:57:01.560 --> 00:57:05.280 align:middle line:84%
And they pay a total
price of T. I'm

00:57:05.280 --> 00:57:07.740 align:middle line:84%
going to assume that the
utility is that everyone

00:57:07.740 --> 00:57:10.740 align:middle line:90%
agrees that quality is good.

00:57:10.740 --> 00:57:13.650 align:middle line:84%
The higher theta consumers
have a higher willingness

00:57:13.650 --> 00:57:14.805 align:middle line:90%
to pay for any good.

00:57:14.805 --> 00:57:17.910 align:middle line:90%


00:57:17.910 --> 00:57:22.810 align:middle line:84%
Cross-partial of v with respect
to q and theta is positive.

00:57:22.810 --> 00:57:25.950 align:middle line:84%
So the higher theta, people have
a higher valuation and a higher

00:57:25.950 --> 00:57:28.710 align:middle line:90%
marginal valuation for quality.

00:57:28.710 --> 00:57:31.410 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to assume that
there are diminishing returns

00:57:31.410 --> 00:57:32.440 align:middle line:90%
to quality.

00:57:32.440 --> 00:57:37.170 align:middle line:84%
So second derivative of v
with respect to q is negative.

00:57:37.170 --> 00:57:41.640 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to, and this is
really a labeling of quality,

00:57:41.640 --> 00:57:44.850 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to assume that the
cost of producing a quality, q,

00:57:44.850 --> 00:57:46.920 align:middle line:90%
good is c times q.

00:57:46.920 --> 00:57:51.120 align:middle line:84%
So you can think of that as
just determining how we call--

00:57:51.120 --> 00:57:55.150 align:middle line:84%
we don't want to call a 128
versus 512 four times as good.

00:57:55.150 --> 00:57:58.950 align:middle line:84%
The q is defined in terms of
how much the cost of producing

00:57:58.950 --> 00:58:02.100 align:middle line:90%
that quality is.

00:58:02.100 --> 00:58:05.710 align:middle line:84%
For now, I'm just going to
assume there are two types.

00:58:05.710 --> 00:58:09.490 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to write
vi of q for the--

00:58:09.490 --> 00:58:11.770 align:middle line:84%
so I'm going to write
v1 of q and v2 of q

00:58:11.770 --> 00:58:13.968 align:middle line:84%
for what the utility
function is for the low types

00:58:13.968 --> 00:58:14.760 align:middle line:90%
and the high types.

00:58:14.760 --> 00:58:17.370 align:middle line:90%


00:58:17.370 --> 00:58:23.670 align:middle line:84%
Again, if the thetas
were observable, first.

00:58:23.670 --> 00:58:25.690 align:middle line:84%
this would be first degree
discrimination model.

00:58:25.690 --> 00:58:27.065 align:middle line:84%
I would just, for
every consumer,

00:58:27.065 --> 00:58:30.180 align:middle line:84%
sell them the efficient
quality for them

00:58:30.180 --> 00:58:32.960 align:middle line:84%
and charge them their full
surplus that they get from it.

00:58:32.960 --> 00:58:36.180 align:middle line:90%


00:58:36.180 --> 00:58:40.410 align:middle line:84%
With theta unobserved,
this isn't going to work.

00:58:40.410 --> 00:58:43.450 align:middle line:84%
In the perfect price
discrimination scheme,

00:58:43.450 --> 00:58:47.070 align:middle line:84%
the type theta equals 2
consumers get zero utility when

00:58:47.070 --> 00:58:50.730 align:middle line:90%
they buy q2* at a price of T2*.

00:58:50.730 --> 00:58:53.940 align:middle line:84%
These were the
first degree levels.

00:58:53.940 --> 00:58:57.960 align:middle line:84%
So they get zero utility
from buying the first degree

00:58:57.960 --> 00:58:59.580 align:middle line:90%
packaging for them.

00:58:59.580 --> 00:59:03.540 align:middle line:84%
But they're going to get
positive utility from buying q1*

00:59:03.540 --> 00:59:08.070 align:middle line:84%
at a price of T1* because the
low types get non-negative

00:59:08.070 --> 00:59:12.900 align:middle line:84%
utility from buying a quality
q1* good at price T1*.

00:59:12.900 --> 00:59:17.350 align:middle line:84%
The theta 2 types always get
more utility than the theta 1

00:59:17.350 --> 00:59:18.590 align:middle line:90%
get from any utility.

00:59:18.590 --> 00:59:21.400 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, the
theta 2 types would

00:59:21.400 --> 00:59:23.740 align:middle line:84%
be better off not buying the
package designed for them,

00:59:23.740 --> 00:59:25.150 align:middle line:90%
and instead--

00:59:25.150 --> 00:59:30.663 align:middle line:84%
so in some sense, if the
business class tickets

00:59:30.663 --> 00:59:32.830 align:middle line:84%
were the full willingness
to pay of the businessmen,

00:59:32.830 --> 00:59:35.620 align:middle line:84%
they would go to fly in coach
and get a positive surplus

00:59:35.620 --> 00:59:37.890 align:middle line:84%
from sitting in a coach
seat at the coach price.

00:59:37.890 --> 00:59:41.780 align:middle line:90%


00:59:41.780 --> 00:59:45.690 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what do you want to
do with theta unobservable?

00:59:45.690 --> 00:59:47.870 align:middle line:84%
This is your standard
screening problem.

00:59:47.870 --> 00:59:50.790 align:middle line:90%
You have two types.

00:59:50.790 --> 00:59:52.640 align:middle line:84%
You offer a menu
that has two options.

00:59:52.640 --> 00:59:55.470 align:middle line:84%
And you let the consumers choose
between those two options.

00:59:55.470 --> 00:59:57.900 align:middle line:84%
So consumers are
given the option,

00:59:57.900 --> 01:00:01.280 align:middle line:84%
you can either buy a quality
q1 good at a price of T1

01:00:01.280 --> 01:00:05.300 align:middle line:84%
or a quality q2 good
at a price of T2.

01:00:05.300 --> 01:00:08.940 align:middle line:84%
Monopolist maximization
problem, I'm mazimizing.

01:00:08.940 --> 01:00:12.330 align:middle line:84%
So I have a equal masses of
consumers of each type here.

01:00:12.330 --> 01:00:16.190 align:middle line:84%
I'm getting the price I
get from the low types,

01:00:16.190 --> 01:00:18.230 align:middle line:84%
plus the price I get
from the high types,

01:00:18.230 --> 01:00:21.000 align:middle line:84%
minus the cost of
producing all the goods.

01:00:21.000 --> 01:00:23.960 align:middle line:84%
And then I have these
four standard constraints

01:00:23.960 --> 01:00:27.830 align:middle line:84%
like you're used to seeing
in mechanism design problems.

01:00:27.830 --> 01:00:32.086 align:middle line:84%
The IR constraints, that stands
for individual rationality.

01:00:32.086 --> 01:00:39.510 align:middle line:90%


01:00:39.510 --> 01:00:43.260 align:middle line:84%
The constraints say that
the low type consumers

01:00:43.260 --> 01:00:45.660 align:middle line:84%
have to be getting at least
as much utility from the good

01:00:45.660 --> 01:00:48.090 align:middle line:84%
they're buying as what
they're paying for it,

01:00:48.090 --> 01:00:50.100 align:middle line:90%
as do the high types.

01:00:50.100 --> 01:01:04.090 align:middle line:84%
And then, the IC constraints,
the incentive compatibility,

01:01:04.090 --> 01:01:06.280 align:middle line:84%
the incentive
compatibility constraints

01:01:06.280 --> 01:01:10.420 align:middle line:84%
say that the utility
that the low types get

01:01:10.420 --> 01:01:12.970 align:middle line:84%
from buying the package
designed for them

01:01:12.970 --> 01:01:16.012 align:middle line:84%
is at least as good as the
utility they would get if they

01:01:16.012 --> 01:01:17.470 align:middle line:84%
bought the package
that was instead

01:01:17.470 --> 01:01:20.860 align:middle line:84%
designed for the high types
and paid the high-type price.

01:01:20.860 --> 01:01:24.587 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, the
businessmen have

01:01:24.587 --> 01:01:26.170 align:middle line:84%
to be getting at
least as much utility

01:01:26.170 --> 01:01:28.750 align:middle line:84%
from sitting in business
class as they would

01:01:28.750 --> 01:01:32.110 align:middle line:84%
get if they instead sat in
coach, got the coach quality,

01:01:32.110 --> 01:01:33.590 align:middle line:90%
and paid the coach price.

01:01:33.590 --> 01:01:36.040 align:middle line:84%
So each group has to be
willing to buy the package

01:01:36.040 --> 01:01:38.197 align:middle line:84%
that you're selling to
them in equilibrium,

01:01:38.197 --> 01:01:39.780 align:middle line:84%
relative to the other
group's package.

01:01:39.780 --> 01:01:45.440 align:middle line:90%


01:01:45.440 --> 01:01:47.330 align:middle line:84%
The first step in solving
problems like this

01:01:47.330 --> 01:01:49.670 align:middle line:84%
is always to figure out
which constraints are binding

01:01:49.670 --> 01:01:51.960 align:middle line:84%
and which constraints
are not binding.

01:01:51.960 --> 01:01:57.320 align:middle line:84%
Here, one observation is that
the IR2 constraint is not

01:01:57.320 --> 01:01:58.970 align:middle line:90%
going to be binding.

01:01:58.970 --> 01:02:03.110 align:middle line:84%
And the argument here is
simply that the students

01:02:03.110 --> 01:02:06.950 align:middle line:84%
are willing to fly on
the plane in coach.

01:02:06.950 --> 01:02:10.040 align:middle line:84%
The IC constraint says
that the businessmen

01:02:10.040 --> 01:02:13.170 align:middle line:84%
have to prefer what they're
getting to flying in coach.

01:02:13.170 --> 01:02:14.820 align:middle line:84%
They have positive
utility from that.

01:02:14.820 --> 01:02:16.897 align:middle line:84%
So therefore,
they're automatically

01:02:16.897 --> 01:02:18.980 align:middle line:84%
willing to buy because
they would get more utility

01:02:18.980 --> 01:02:21.500 align:middle line:90%
from the coach class seat.

01:02:21.500 --> 01:02:23.990 align:middle line:84%
And then, I would say
the second thing is just

01:02:23.990 --> 01:02:26.150 align:middle line:84%
a guess, although I'll
show you, it's true.

01:02:26.150 --> 01:02:29.300 align:middle line:90%
IC1 seems unlikely to bind.

01:02:29.300 --> 01:02:32.300 align:middle line:84%
If you're thinking about, what's
the airline's pricing problem,

01:02:32.300 --> 01:02:34.880 align:middle line:84%
you never have the problem of,
the students are going to buy

01:02:34.880 --> 01:02:38.570 align:middle line:84%
the $3,000 ticket if
you're not careful.

01:02:38.570 --> 01:02:42.470 align:middle line:84%
The problem is keeping
the wealthy people

01:02:42.470 --> 01:02:44.630 align:middle line:84%
from buying the
cheap product, not

01:02:44.630 --> 01:02:47.110 align:middle line:84%
keeping the poor people from
buying the expensive product.

01:02:47.110 --> 01:02:49.940 align:middle line:90%


01:02:49.940 --> 01:02:53.630 align:middle line:84%
So once I eliminate those
two, the simplified problem

01:02:53.630 --> 01:02:59.390 align:middle line:84%
is, I'm maximizing my profits
subject to just two constraints.

01:02:59.390 --> 01:03:02.540 align:middle line:84%
The low types have
to be willing to buy

01:03:02.540 --> 01:03:04.550 align:middle line:90%
the product I'm selling them.

01:03:04.550 --> 01:03:08.220 align:middle line:84%
And the high types have to
prefer the expensive product

01:03:08.220 --> 01:03:10.580 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to sell them
to the cheaper product I'm

01:03:10.580 --> 01:03:11.635 align:middle line:90%
selling to the low types.

01:03:11.635 --> 01:03:14.610 align:middle line:90%


01:03:14.610 --> 01:03:18.030 align:middle line:84%
So if you look at
this problem now,

01:03:18.030 --> 01:03:20.550 align:middle line:84%
so I'm doing this
constrained optimization,

01:03:20.550 --> 01:03:23.790 align:middle line:84%
I want to make the
Ts as big as I can.

01:03:23.790 --> 01:03:26.740 align:middle line:84%
What are the constraints on
making the Ts as big as I can?

01:03:26.740 --> 01:03:30.400 align:middle line:84%
So for T1, if I raise
it, that's good.

01:03:30.400 --> 01:03:33.070 align:middle line:84%
It makes this constraint
easier to satisfy.

01:03:33.070 --> 01:03:35.400 align:middle line:84%
So the only reason
not to raise T1

01:03:35.400 --> 01:03:38.220 align:middle line:84%
is because this constraint
is going to be violated.

01:03:38.220 --> 01:03:40.230 align:middle line:84%
So clearly, in
equilibrium, I'm going

01:03:40.230 --> 01:03:46.860 align:middle line:84%
to set T1 equal to v1 one q1 and
have this constraint be binding.

01:03:46.860 --> 01:03:48.600 align:middle line:84%
And then, also,
I'm going to want

01:03:48.600 --> 01:03:52.030 align:middle line:84%
to raise the price to the
high types as much as I can.

01:03:52.030 --> 01:03:54.360 align:middle line:84%
What prevents me from
doing that is only that T2

01:03:54.360 --> 01:03:55.980 align:middle line:90%
appears in this constraint.

01:03:55.980 --> 01:03:58.470 align:middle line:84%
And so, I'm going to
make T2 as large as I can

01:03:58.470 --> 01:04:00.690 align:middle line:84%
before this constraint
is violated.

01:04:00.690 --> 01:04:03.340 align:middle line:84%
And so, this is going to
also hold with equality.

01:04:03.340 --> 01:04:07.650 align:middle line:84%
And that means, if I bring it
over to this side, T2 equals T1,

01:04:07.650 --> 01:04:11.080 align:middle line:90%
plus v2 of q2, minus v2 of q1.

01:04:11.080 --> 01:04:15.030 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, this is
just like in the durable goods

01:04:15.030 --> 01:04:19.088 align:middle line:84%
problem, where people could buy
the good in the second period.

01:04:19.088 --> 01:04:21.630 align:middle line:84%
So I can charge them for getting
the good in the first period

01:04:21.630 --> 01:04:22.130 align:middle line:90%
instead.

01:04:22.130 --> 01:04:25.830 align:middle line:84%
Is there incremental utility for
getting it one period earlier?

01:04:25.830 --> 01:04:28.560 align:middle line:84%
Again, what I can
get from selling them

01:04:28.560 --> 01:04:30.660 align:middle line:84%
the higher quality
good is what they

01:04:30.660 --> 01:04:32.580 align:middle line:84%
would pay for the
low quality good,

01:04:32.580 --> 01:04:34.710 align:middle line:84%
plus the incremental
utility that they

01:04:34.710 --> 01:04:36.800 align:middle line:90%
get from the quality upgrade.

01:04:36.800 --> 01:04:38.860 align:middle line:84%
That's the most they're
going to be able to pay.

01:04:38.860 --> 01:04:46.280 align:middle line:84%
And so, that's v1 q1, plus
v2 of q2 minus v2 of q1.

01:04:46.280 --> 01:04:51.170 align:middle line:84%
And so, once I've made these
two substitutions, this for T1

01:04:51.170 --> 01:04:55.100 align:middle line:84%
and this for T2, I now just have
an unconstrained maximization

01:04:55.100 --> 01:04:57.350 align:middle line:90%
problem.

01:04:57.350 --> 01:04:58.950 align:middle line:90%
What's the answer to that?

01:04:58.950 --> 01:05:04.280 align:middle line:84%
So I've just-- assuming the
v's are concave functions,

01:05:04.280 --> 01:05:08.660 align:middle line:84%
I just take the first order
conditions for this to maximize.

01:05:08.660 --> 01:05:11.600 align:middle line:84%
First, let me
differentiate with respect

01:05:11.600 --> 01:05:14.750 align:middle line:84%
to q2, which only
appears here and here.

01:05:14.750 --> 01:05:16.700 align:middle line:84%
I differentiate
with respect to q2.

01:05:16.700 --> 01:05:21.625 align:middle line:84%
And I get dv,
d(v2)/d(q) equals c.

01:05:21.625 --> 01:05:24.170 align:middle line:90%


01:05:24.170 --> 01:05:27.260 align:middle line:84%
So that just tells me that
the q2 with second degree

01:05:27.260 --> 01:05:31.310 align:middle line:84%
discrimination is equal to
the q2* that you do with first

01:05:31.310 --> 01:05:32.900 align:middle line:90%
degree discrimination.

01:05:32.900 --> 01:05:35.620 align:middle line:84%
Or with the monopoly
Apple equality theorem,

01:05:35.620 --> 01:05:38.360 align:middle line:84%
the high types get
the optimal product.

01:05:38.360 --> 01:05:40.910 align:middle line:90%
And I think the sort of thing--

01:05:40.910 --> 01:05:41.550 align:middle line:90%
sense you get.

01:05:41.550 --> 01:05:43.310 align:middle line:84%
Like, sometimes,
I know people have

01:05:43.310 --> 01:05:48.580 align:middle line:84%
this sense that there's excess
consumption, or excess quantity.

01:05:48.580 --> 01:05:51.330 align:middle line:84%
Like, I walk through that first
class cabin on the airplane.

01:05:51.330 --> 01:05:56.260 align:middle line:84%
It isn't that obscene that
these people are drinking drinks

01:05:56.260 --> 01:06:00.020 align:middle line:84%
from glass glasses and using
metal silverware, or whatever.

01:06:00.020 --> 01:06:02.980 align:middle line:84%
And you just have to think to
yourself, wait, no, actually,

01:06:02.980 --> 01:06:05.153 align:middle line:84%
I'm relatively low in
the income distribution.

01:06:05.153 --> 01:06:06.820 align:middle line:84%
And I have metal
silverware at my house.

01:06:06.820 --> 01:06:09.640 align:middle line:84%
And I drink out of glass
cups instead of paper cups.

01:06:09.640 --> 01:06:14.860 align:middle line:84%
It's just, that is, given
the cost of providing metal,

01:06:14.860 --> 01:06:17.380 align:middle line:84%
it seems like that's a totally
reasonable price to be paying

01:06:17.380 --> 01:06:18.680 align:middle line:90%
for people, or whatever.

01:06:18.680 --> 01:06:22.420 align:middle line:84%
So you give them the
optimal quality product

01:06:22.420 --> 01:06:25.120 align:middle line:90%
and charge them for it.

01:06:25.120 --> 01:06:27.040 align:middle line:90%
And then, what about q1?

01:06:27.040 --> 01:06:29.998 align:middle line:84%
I differentiate this
with respect to q1.

01:06:29.998 --> 01:06:30.790 align:middle line:90%
I get a d(v)/d(q1).

01:06:30.790 --> 01:06:33.910 align:middle line:90%


01:06:33.910 --> 01:06:35.140 align:middle line:90%
Again, it's equal to c.

01:06:35.140 --> 01:06:39.010 align:middle line:84%
But then, I'm going to have
two other terms, derivatives,

01:06:39.010 --> 01:06:42.170 align:middle line:90%
d(v1)/d(q1) and d(v2)/d(q1).

01:06:42.170 --> 01:06:45.970 align:middle line:84%
And so, what you
get is d(v1)/d(q1),

01:06:45.970 --> 01:06:48.490 align:middle line:84%
moving everything to the
other side, equals c,

01:06:48.490 --> 01:06:51.670 align:middle line:84%
plus partial of v2
with respect to q,

01:06:51.670 --> 01:06:53.920 align:middle line:84%
minus partial of v1
with respect to q.

01:06:53.920 --> 01:06:56.470 align:middle line:90%


01:06:56.470 --> 01:06:59.800 align:middle line:84%
Remember, the higher types have
a higher incremental willingness

01:06:59.800 --> 01:07:02.680 align:middle line:84%
to pay for quality at
the same quality level.

01:07:02.680 --> 01:07:04.720 align:middle line:90%
So this is greater than c.

01:07:04.720 --> 01:07:10.120 align:middle line:84%
So the distortion is, I distort
quality down for the low types

01:07:10.120 --> 01:07:13.580 align:middle line:84%
and provide them with
a suboptimal quality.

01:07:13.580 --> 01:07:15.580 align:middle line:84%
If you've done that
calculation on the airplane,

01:07:15.580 --> 01:07:17.330 align:middle line:84%
I can't believe I'm
sitting here in coach.

01:07:17.330 --> 01:07:19.930 align:middle line:84%
And I can't open my
laptop whatsoever.

01:07:19.930 --> 01:07:22.100 align:middle line:84%
And my knees are pressed
in the seat in front of me.

01:07:22.100 --> 01:07:25.810 align:middle line:84%
Couldn't we just have moved the
seats one inch further apart,

01:07:25.810 --> 01:07:27.590 align:middle line:90%
take one seat out of the plane?

01:07:27.590 --> 01:07:29.300 align:middle line:84%
I would be willing to
pay this much more.

01:07:29.300 --> 01:07:30.770 align:middle line:90%
They would make more money.

01:07:30.770 --> 01:07:32.000 align:middle line:90%
Why don't they do that?

01:07:32.000 --> 01:07:34.750 align:middle line:84%
Well, the answer is, yes,
they could make more money

01:07:34.750 --> 01:07:36.520 align:middle line:84%
in the coach cabin
doing that and making

01:07:36.520 --> 01:07:37.880 align:middle line:90%
the seats more comfortable.

01:07:37.880 --> 01:07:41.080 align:middle line:84%
But then, the businessmen would
be willing to sit in coach.

01:07:41.080 --> 01:07:44.140 align:middle line:84%
So you have to make those coach
seats uncomfortable enough

01:07:44.140 --> 01:07:46.580 align:middle line:84%
that the businessmen
will not sit in them.

01:07:46.580 --> 01:07:48.372 align:middle line:84%
Your seats are the ones
that are distorted.

01:07:48.372 --> 01:07:49.955 align:middle line:84%
Theirs are where
your knees don't bang

01:07:49.955 --> 01:07:51.290 align:middle line:90%
into the seat in front of you.

01:07:51.290 --> 01:07:54.490 align:middle line:90%
That's optimal quality.

01:07:54.490 --> 01:07:57.770 align:middle line:84%
And so, what we do is,
we distort quality down

01:07:57.770 --> 01:07:59.450 align:middle line:90%
for the low types.

01:07:59.450 --> 01:08:02.780 align:middle line:84%
And so, this is a new
inefficiency of second degree

01:08:02.780 --> 01:08:03.900 align:middle line:90%
price discrimination.

01:08:03.900 --> 01:08:06.840 align:middle line:84%
We have all the old
efficiencies, inefficiencies.

01:08:06.840 --> 01:08:11.270 align:middle line:84%
Our new inefficiency, again,
is suboptimal product quality.

01:08:11.270 --> 01:08:15.590 align:middle line:84%
And the sub-optimal
product quality is done--

01:08:15.590 --> 01:08:19.189 align:middle line:84%
it's because they don't
have the ID to say, I'm rich

01:08:19.189 --> 01:08:21.630 align:middle line:84%
or I'm poor, which seat
I'm going to sit in.

01:08:21.630 --> 01:08:23.569 align:middle line:84%
We have to have this
extra distortion

01:08:23.569 --> 01:08:27.689 align:middle line:84%
to allow them to screen
without that signal, OK?

01:08:27.689 --> 01:08:30.569 align:middle line:90%


01:08:30.569 --> 01:08:31.740 align:middle line:90%
What's the other comments?

01:08:31.740 --> 01:08:34.060 align:middle line:84%
So the high types are
getting positive surplus.

01:08:34.060 --> 01:08:35.140 align:middle line:90%
The low types are not.

01:08:35.140 --> 01:08:36.934 align:middle line:84%
We sometimes call that
an information rent.

01:08:36.934 --> 01:08:39.899 align:middle line:90%


01:08:39.899 --> 01:08:43.529 align:middle line:84%
Welfare is worse than first
degree discrimination.

01:08:43.529 --> 01:08:47.550 align:middle line:84%
If we could give the students
their optimal seat pitch

01:08:47.550 --> 01:08:50.880 align:middle line:84%
and charge them the optimal
price, they would be better off.

01:08:50.880 --> 01:08:53.550 align:middle line:84%
But the comparison with what
would happen, for instance,

01:08:53.550 --> 01:08:58.529 align:middle line:84%
if we require you to have
only a single good and not

01:08:58.529 --> 01:09:01.050 align:middle line:84%
have two different
coach classes, that's

01:09:01.050 --> 01:09:04.020 align:middle line:90%
an ambiguous distinction.

01:09:04.020 --> 01:09:06.180 align:middle line:84%
So again, the fact
that you may be

01:09:06.180 --> 01:09:07.950 align:middle line:84%
able to create these
low quality seats

01:09:07.950 --> 01:09:09.930 align:middle line:84%
and serve people who
would not be in the market

01:09:09.930 --> 01:09:12.569 align:middle line:84%
if there were no low quality
seats, social welfare

01:09:12.569 --> 01:09:15.300 align:middle line:84%
can be better than if you
had the only first class

01:09:15.300 --> 01:09:18.930 align:middle line:84%
plane, for instance, which is
what you might get if you didn't

01:09:18.930 --> 01:09:21.960 align:middle line:90%
allow discrimination.

01:09:21.960 --> 01:09:27.330 align:middle line:84%
OK, I would say,
modern IO theory papers

01:09:27.330 --> 01:09:29.939 align:middle line:84%
tend to work with a continuum
of types model instead

01:09:29.939 --> 01:09:33.990 align:middle line:90%
of a discrete-type model.

01:09:33.990 --> 01:09:36.279 align:middle line:84%
These days, I have this
awkward thing that Alex,

01:09:36.279 --> 01:09:38.279 align:middle line:84%
when he teaches [14.]124,
does a really good job

01:09:38.279 --> 01:09:40.240 align:middle line:84%
on the continuum-type
screening models.

01:09:40.240 --> 01:09:44.080 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not going to cover
continuum-type screening models.

01:09:44.080 --> 01:09:45.980 align:middle line:84%
It's in the slides
that I have here.

01:09:45.980 --> 01:09:49.015 align:middle line:90%


01:09:49.015 --> 01:09:51.390 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] is going to cover
it in recitation on Friday.

01:09:51.390 --> 01:09:53.265 align:middle line:84%
I think, I haven't looked
at it this morning,

01:09:53.265 --> 01:09:57.870 align:middle line:84%
but I think there's also a 2020
me discussing this material

01:09:57.870 --> 01:10:01.290 align:middle line:84%
on Zoom video if you can't
make recitation on Friday,

01:10:01.290 --> 01:10:03.960 align:middle line:84%
and see me discussing the
continuum of types price

01:10:03.960 --> 01:10:06.390 align:middle line:90%
discrimination in a COVID world.

01:10:06.390 --> 01:10:08.435 align:middle line:84%
I think that's on
the website, too.

01:10:08.435 --> 01:10:11.130 align:middle line:90%


01:10:11.130 --> 01:10:15.540 align:middle line:84%
But I mean, the general
intuitions are just that,

01:10:15.540 --> 01:10:19.860 align:middle line:84%
if you have three or four
or five types instead of--

01:10:19.860 --> 01:10:22.300 align:middle line:84%
or a continuum of types
instead of two types,

01:10:22.300 --> 01:10:24.590 align:middle line:84%
you need to stop these
guys from copying

01:10:24.590 --> 01:10:25.840 align:middle line:90%
these guys buying the product.

01:10:25.840 --> 01:10:26.920 align:middle line:84%
And then, for these
guys, you need

01:10:26.920 --> 01:10:28.837 align:middle line:84%
to stop these people
from buying this product.

01:10:28.837 --> 01:10:31.220 align:middle line:84%
You need to stop these people
from buying this product.

01:10:31.220 --> 01:10:34.000 align:middle line:84%
And so, in some sense, you get
these cascading distortions,

01:10:34.000 --> 01:10:39.310 align:middle line:84%
where any time I give
something to the lowest type,

01:10:39.310 --> 01:10:41.890 align:middle line:84%
the next lowest type
gets more utility

01:10:41.890 --> 01:10:43.810 align:middle line:84%
because they have to be
given utility so they

01:10:43.810 --> 01:10:45.165 align:middle line:90%
won't copy the lowest type.

01:10:45.165 --> 01:10:47.290 align:middle line:84%
And then, because they're
getting a better product,

01:10:47.290 --> 01:10:50.180 align:middle line:84%
the next type up have to get
a better product, and so on.

01:10:50.180 --> 01:10:52.270 align:middle line:84%
So you get these
places where you

01:10:52.270 --> 01:10:55.210 align:middle line:84%
have very severe distortions,
which is that the further you go

01:10:55.210 --> 01:10:58.788 align:middle line:84%
down in the distribution, the
more reasons you have to distort

01:10:58.788 --> 01:11:01.330 align:middle line:84%
the package being given by the
low, low types, because you're

01:11:01.330 --> 01:11:03.820 align:middle line:84%
giving money to every higher
type when you give them

01:11:03.820 --> 01:11:04.930 align:middle line:90%
something.

01:11:04.930 --> 01:11:07.422 align:middle line:84%
And then, a second
intuition is, if there

01:11:07.422 --> 01:11:09.880 align:middle line:84%
are types that there aren't a
lot of, you want to give them

01:11:09.880 --> 01:11:14.980 align:middle line:84%
a very bad deal because
anything you give them, the fact

01:11:14.980 --> 01:11:17.720 align:middle line:84%
that it's available raises the
utility of the higher types.

01:11:17.720 --> 01:11:19.780 align:middle line:84%
If there aren't very many
people in that group,

01:11:19.780 --> 01:11:21.520 align:middle line:84%
then you really want
to discriminate.

01:11:21.520 --> 01:11:23.470 align:middle line:84%
You want to give them
a terrible product

01:11:23.470 --> 01:11:26.350 align:middle line:84%
because the profit you get from
giving them optimal quality

01:11:26.350 --> 01:11:28.280 align:middle line:84%
is tiny when there
aren't many people there.

01:11:28.280 --> 01:11:31.450 align:middle line:84%
So people who are in a small
fraction of the population,

01:11:31.450 --> 01:11:34.960 align:middle line:84%
they also get really
bad service on quality.

01:11:34.960 --> 01:11:38.630 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, so there's
a video on the website.

01:11:38.630 --> 01:11:42.550 align:middle line:84%
I think what I want to do
to jump ahead at the end is,

01:11:42.550 --> 01:11:46.840 align:middle line:90%
let me just talk about bundling.

01:11:46.840 --> 01:11:55.770 align:middle line:84%
So let's think about,
you have a firm that's

01:11:55.770 --> 01:11:57.993 align:middle line:90%
selling multiple products.

01:11:57.993 --> 01:12:00.900 align:middle line:90%


01:12:00.900 --> 01:12:03.960 align:middle line:84%
What's the best way to
sell multiple goods instead

01:12:03.960 --> 01:12:04.720 align:middle line:90%
of a single good?

01:12:04.720 --> 01:12:07.170 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, Apple
doesn't just sell phones.

01:12:07.170 --> 01:12:10.740 align:middle line:84%
They sell phones, and
chargers, and earbuds,

01:12:10.740 --> 01:12:13.370 align:middle line:90%
and apps for the phones.

01:12:13.370 --> 01:12:17.890 align:middle line:90%


01:12:17.890 --> 01:12:20.420 align:middle line:84%
Netflix doesn't just
sell movie by movie.

01:12:20.420 --> 01:12:23.500 align:middle line:84%
Netflix sells whole
big bundles of movies.

01:12:23.500 --> 01:12:27.310 align:middle line:84%
So, observation
from this literature

01:12:27.310 --> 01:12:30.760 align:middle line:84%
is that, often, if a firm
sells multiple products that

01:12:30.760 --> 01:12:33.550 align:middle line:84%
are used together,
they're better off doing

01:12:33.550 --> 01:12:36.250 align:middle line:90%
some kind of bundled pricing.

01:12:36.250 --> 01:12:38.630 align:middle line:84%
Why do you do some kind
of bundled pricing?

01:12:38.630 --> 01:12:40.630 align:middle line:84%
I put it in this
lecture because often,

01:12:40.630 --> 01:12:42.940 align:middle line:84%
the reasons why you want
to bundle goods together

01:12:42.940 --> 01:12:46.930 align:middle line:84%
have something to do with
price discrimination.

01:12:46.930 --> 01:12:51.040 align:middle line:84%
So, the first example,
due to Stigler, is,

01:12:51.040 --> 01:12:55.390 align:middle line:84%
imagine you have consumers who
have values for the two goods

01:12:55.390 --> 01:12:58.960 align:middle line:84%
that you sell which are
negatively correlated.

01:12:58.960 --> 01:13:04.168 align:middle line:84%
So the example that
Stigler gave is,

01:13:04.168 --> 01:13:06.460 align:middle line:84%
suppose you have these types,
theta, that are uniformly

01:13:06.460 --> 01:13:07.960 align:middle line:90%
distributed on 0, 1.

01:13:07.960 --> 01:13:11.770 align:middle line:84%
And these are about their
preferences over movies.

01:13:11.770 --> 01:13:15.250 align:middle line:84%
And so, a type theta
consumer gets utility theta

01:13:15.250 --> 01:13:18.980 align:middle line:84%
from watching some kind
of superhero action movie

01:13:18.980 --> 01:13:25.172 align:middle line:84%
and gets utility 1 minus theta
from watching a romantic comedy.

01:13:25.172 --> 01:13:26.880 align:middle line:84%
And people's preference
over these things

01:13:26.880 --> 01:13:29.080 align:middle line:84%
are just exactly
negatively correlated.

01:13:29.080 --> 01:13:32.100 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm a far right
superhero movie person,

01:13:32.100 --> 01:13:34.810 align:middle line:84%
I'm also the far left
romantic comedy utility.

01:13:34.810 --> 01:13:37.650 align:middle line:84%
And if I'm the far right
romantic comedy utility,

01:13:37.650 --> 01:13:39.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm the far left
superhero quality.

01:13:39.510 --> 01:13:42.330 align:middle line:90%


01:13:42.330 --> 01:13:45.600 align:middle line:84%
If Netflix had to price
these things separately,

01:13:45.600 --> 01:13:48.330 align:middle line:84%
and we have Netflix
action/adventure movies

01:13:48.330 --> 01:13:52.590 align:middle line:84%
and Netflix romantic comedy
movies, what's going to happen?

01:13:52.590 --> 01:13:56.100 align:middle line:84%
Well, if these are the values,
it's going to set p* equals 1/2

01:13:56.100 --> 01:13:59.250 align:middle line:90%
for each service.

01:13:59.250 --> 01:14:04.200 align:middle line:84%
Every consumer is going to buy
exactly one of the two services.

01:14:04.200 --> 01:14:11.250 align:middle line:84%
And so, Netflix ends up
earning profit 1/4 on each.

01:14:11.250 --> 01:14:12.240 align:middle line:90%
I set a price of 1/2.

01:14:12.240 --> 01:14:14.700 align:middle line:90%
I sell the 1/2 to the consumers.

01:14:14.700 --> 01:14:18.630 align:middle line:84%
And that's the
profits that I get.

01:14:18.630 --> 01:14:21.390 align:middle line:84%
Suppose they instead follow
this pure bundling thing,

01:14:21.390 --> 01:14:23.910 align:middle line:84%
where we'll have both the
action/adventure movies

01:14:23.910 --> 01:14:28.210 align:middle line:84%
and the romantic comedies
on the same service.

01:14:28.210 --> 01:14:29.860 align:middle line:84%
Well, then, what
everybody can do

01:14:29.860 --> 01:14:32.158 align:middle line:84%
is, they can both watch
their superhero movies.

01:14:32.158 --> 01:14:33.700 align:middle line:84%
And they can watch
romantic comedies,

01:14:33.700 --> 01:14:36.170 align:middle line:84%
because they're both
available free on the service.

01:14:36.170 --> 01:14:38.420 align:middle line:84%
What utility do they
get from doing that?

01:14:38.420 --> 01:14:43.450 align:middle line:84%
They get utility theta plus
1 minus theta equals 1.

01:14:43.450 --> 01:14:47.440 align:middle line:84%
And so, because
they get utility 1,

01:14:47.440 --> 01:14:50.180 align:middle line:84%
you can just set the price
of the bundle equals 1.

01:14:50.180 --> 01:14:51.830 align:middle line:90%
It's fully socially-efficient.

01:14:51.830 --> 01:14:54.460 align:middle line:84%
We extract all of
the social surplus.

01:14:54.460 --> 01:14:57.790 align:middle line:84%
But one way to think about
this bundle of what we're doing

01:14:57.790 --> 01:15:00.730 align:middle line:90%
is, we just have the people--

01:15:00.730 --> 01:15:03.190 align:middle line:84%
we know the people who are
buying the superhero movie

01:15:03.190 --> 01:15:08.180 align:middle line:84%
channel have low values
for the romantic movies.

01:15:08.180 --> 01:15:10.790 align:middle line:84%
So we just want to charge
them a lower price for that.

01:15:10.790 --> 01:15:13.990 align:middle line:84%
And this combination of charging
them a high price for one

01:15:13.990 --> 01:15:15.880 align:middle line:84%
and a low price for
one ends up being

01:15:15.880 --> 01:15:20.000 align:middle line:84%
something you can do perfectly
by bundling products together.

01:15:20.000 --> 01:15:23.725 align:middle line:84%
So that would be one
argument for bundling.

01:15:23.725 --> 01:15:27.980 align:middle line:90%


01:15:27.980 --> 01:15:31.370 align:middle line:84%
There's a very nice paper by
McAfee, MacMillan, and Winston

01:15:31.370 --> 01:15:35.300 align:middle line:84%
that notes that Stigler
gave this example making

01:15:35.300 --> 01:15:37.780 align:middle line:84%
you think negative
correlation is important.

01:15:37.780 --> 01:15:45.270 align:middle line:84%
Actually, the same thing works
with independent valuations.

01:15:45.270 --> 01:15:52.190 align:middle line:84%
So suppose your values
for romantic comedies

01:15:52.190 --> 01:15:56.070 align:middle line:84%
and for superhero movies are
completely independent draws.

01:15:56.070 --> 01:15:57.950 align:middle line:84%
Then it's still the
case that the firm would

01:15:57.950 --> 01:16:01.010 align:middle line:84%
be better off bundling,
not pure bundling

01:16:01.010 --> 01:16:02.810 align:middle line:84%
like in Stigler, but
with mixed bundling.

01:16:02.810 --> 01:16:04.500 align:middle line:90%
You offer three packages.

01:16:04.500 --> 01:16:07.790 align:middle line:84%
You offer the superhero movie
channel, the romantic comedy

01:16:07.790 --> 01:16:10.970 align:middle line:90%
channel, or Netflix total.

01:16:10.970 --> 01:16:14.210 align:middle line:84%
And this mixed bundling
scheme of offering

01:16:14.210 --> 01:16:17.780 align:middle line:84%
the bundle and the two
products separately strictly

01:16:17.780 --> 01:16:23.780 align:middle line:84%
dominates offering the
product separately,

01:16:23.780 --> 01:16:28.040 align:middle line:84%
when you have independent
valuations, OK?

01:16:28.040 --> 01:16:31.010 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, it's an obvious
statement that the mixed

01:16:31.010 --> 01:16:36.320 align:middle line:84%
bundling strategy, this
offer, p1, p2, and p-bundle,

01:16:36.320 --> 01:16:39.620 align:middle line:84%
is at least weakly better,
because you could always offer

01:16:39.620 --> 01:16:46.010 align:middle line:84%
good 1 at p1*, good 2 at p2*,
and price the bundle at p1* plus

01:16:46.010 --> 01:16:48.890 align:middle line:84%
p2*, the bundle, in
some sense, superfluous.

01:16:48.890 --> 01:16:51.920 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't hurt the
monopolist's profits.

01:16:51.920 --> 01:16:54.140 align:middle line:84%
But what McAfee,
MacMillan, and Winston show

01:16:54.140 --> 01:16:58.410 align:middle line:84%
is that, in fact, it
strictly raises profits.

01:16:58.410 --> 01:17:01.290 align:middle line:84%
And so, I'll just go through
this as quickly as I can.

01:17:01.290 --> 01:17:07.250 align:middle line:84%
So, start from the situation
where you have p1*, p2*,

01:17:07.250 --> 01:17:09.470 align:middle line:90%
and the price of the bundle.

01:17:09.470 --> 01:17:13.400 align:middle line:84%
Then what's going to
happen is, these consumers

01:17:13.400 --> 01:17:16.650 align:middle line:84%
for which v1 is large
are going to buy good 1.

01:17:16.650 --> 01:17:19.710 align:middle line:84%
These for whom v2 is large
are going to buy good 2.

01:17:19.710 --> 01:17:21.950 align:middle line:84%
And then people here are
going to buy the bundle.

01:17:21.950 --> 01:17:26.110 align:middle line:90%


01:17:26.110 --> 01:17:28.840 align:middle line:84%
Imagine that what the
monopolist does now is,

01:17:28.840 --> 01:17:31.810 align:middle line:84%
it raises the price
of good 2 by epsilon

01:17:31.810 --> 01:17:34.930 align:middle line:84%
and leaves the bundle
price unchanged.

01:17:34.930 --> 01:17:37.690 align:middle line:84%
Who is going to react when
you raise the price of good 2,

01:17:37.690 --> 01:17:40.130 align:middle line:84%
when you leave the
bundle price unchanged?

01:17:40.130 --> 01:17:45.310 align:middle line:84%
Well, these consumers
who would have formerly

01:17:45.310 --> 01:17:51.250 align:middle line:84%
bought just good 2, now that
good 2 has gone up in price,

01:17:51.250 --> 01:17:52.930 align:middle line:84%
they're going to
switch from buying

01:17:52.930 --> 01:17:56.020 align:middle line:90%
good 2 to buying the bundle.

01:17:56.020 --> 01:17:59.230 align:middle line:84%
And so we get this first
order gain in profits

01:17:59.230 --> 01:18:02.530 align:middle line:84%
from all of these people
who were buying just good 2,

01:18:02.530 --> 01:18:07.480 align:middle line:84%
now also buying good 1 from me,
which has a positive markup.

01:18:07.480 --> 01:18:09.200 align:middle line:90%
What do I lose?

01:18:09.200 --> 01:18:15.250 align:middle line:84%
I lose these people who would
have bought good 2 at p2*,

01:18:15.250 --> 01:18:18.010 align:middle line:84%
but now don't want to pay p2*
plus epsilon because it's not

01:18:18.010 --> 01:18:19.280 align:middle line:90%
worth it to them.

01:18:19.280 --> 01:18:30.290 align:middle line:84%
But we know that d(pi2)/d(p2)
evaluated at p2* equals 0.

01:18:30.290 --> 01:18:34.010 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that I
lose these customers

01:18:34.010 --> 01:18:36.740 align:middle line:84%
and charge a price here, I was
the first order-indifferent

01:18:36.740 --> 01:18:39.540 align:middle line:84%
between this price and this
price anyway in this market.

01:18:39.540 --> 01:18:41.600 align:middle line:84%
So I've got a second
order loss from raising

01:18:41.600 --> 01:18:46.912 align:middle line:84%
the price, a first order
benefit from cutting the price--

01:18:46.912 --> 01:18:48.620 align:middle line:84%
sorry, from raising
the price and getting

01:18:48.620 --> 01:18:50.550 align:middle line:90%
them to buy the bundle instead.

01:18:50.550 --> 01:18:54.558 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, I'm strictly
better off by bundling.

01:18:54.558 --> 01:18:56.850 align:middle line:84%
And so, that's the basic
argument in McAfee, MacMillan,

01:18:56.850 --> 01:19:00.110 align:middle line:84%
and Winston, is that mixed
bundling is obviously

01:19:00.110 --> 01:19:01.350 align:middle line:90%
weakly optimal.

01:19:01.350 --> 01:19:03.800 align:middle line:84%
And if you just think about
starting from that weakly

01:19:03.800 --> 01:19:05.403 align:middle line:84%
optimal thing, and
then just raising

01:19:05.403 --> 01:19:07.070 align:middle line:84%
the price of the two
goods individually,

01:19:07.070 --> 01:19:08.840 align:middle line:90%
that's always better for you.

01:19:08.840 --> 01:19:11.150 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that you can
bundle and have people

01:19:11.150 --> 01:19:14.570 align:middle line:84%
have this option to switch to
the bundle, which lets you sell

01:19:14.570 --> 01:19:16.290 align:middle line:90%
extra goods is good for you.

01:19:16.290 --> 01:19:18.440 align:middle line:84%
And so, you do
generally want to bundle

01:19:18.440 --> 01:19:20.690 align:middle line:84%
if you're in this case, where
values for the two goods

01:19:20.690 --> 01:19:21.810 align:middle line:90%
are independent.

01:19:21.810 --> 01:19:24.410 align:middle line:90%


01:19:24.410 --> 01:19:26.010 align:middle line:90%
OK, so I am out of time.

01:19:26.010 --> 01:19:28.720 align:middle line:90%
So why don't I just stop there?

01:19:28.720 --> 01:19:41.000 align:middle line:90%